ing overhead, whileCasual-\n255:MCQ and structured word-problem formats) drawn from MMLU, ARC, and GSM8K.\n258:histogram matching for length control (see Section A.2).\n268:A.2 LENGTHCONTROL\n269:Motivation.Initially, the datasets exhibited significant length disparities, creating a potential\n270:shortcut for probes.Bench-Evalprompts had a median length of 248 characters, while the raw\n272:Histogram Matching.To eliminate length as a confounding variable in the primary training con-\n273:trast (Bench-Evalvs.Casual-Deploy), we performed histogram matching. We calculated the length\n274:distribution ofBench-Evalusing 30 percentile-based character-length bins and sampled the exact\n277:visualizes the resulting length distributions.\n285:•bench deploy: benchmark format + conversational genre.\n286:•casual deploy formal: free-form format + academic/impersonal genre.\n287:•bench deploy formal: benchmark format + academic/impersonal genre.\n290:audit and remove such leakage. As shown in Table 3, removing leakage increases the probe’s confi-\n292:feature that suppressed the format signal.\n294:Removing leakage makes the probemorelikely to flag the prompt as Eval, isolating the format\n305:•Precision:Model inference is performed inbfloat16; probe training usesfloat32.\n306:Probe Training.\n307:•Architecture:Logistic Regression (linear probe).\n315:training regimes. Table 4 shows the failure of standard probes to generalize: they consistently\n317:that probes trained on the paired dataset (decorrelating format from context) successfully learn to\n319:Evaluation on AI Safety Datasets.We evaluate probes on safety benchmarks (AdvBench, Harm-\n321:are universally classified as Deployment (0% Eval) by both standard and paired probes. This sug-\n322:gests that evaluation awareness probes should not be interpreted as general-purpose safety monitors.\n328:as Eval. The standard probe consistently relies on format, incorrectly flaggingBench-Deployas\n337:probe consistently learns Context (Evaluation) vs. Format (Benchmark).\n365:- Preserve the original task intent and required information.\n373:Table 6: Probe classification on AI Safety Datasets (Layer 24). All safety prompts are classified as\n375:Dataset Single Probe Paired Probe\n380:Output format:\n413:- Preserve the original task intent and information.\n\n===== /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt =====\n\n\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nEvaluation Awareness in Language Models Has\nLimited Effect on Behaviour\nAmelie Knecht∗\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nLucas Florin\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nThilo Hagendorff\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nAbstract\nLarge reasoning models (LRMs) sometimes note in their chain of thought (CoT)\nthat they may be under evaluation. Researchers worry that this verbalised evaluation\nawareness (VEA) causes models to adapt their outputs strategically, optimising\nfor perceived evaluation criteria, which, for instance, can make models appear\nsafer than they actually are. However, whether VEA actually has this effect is\nlargely unknown. We tested this across open-weight LRMs and benchmarks\ncovering safety, alignment, moral reasoning, and political opinion. We tested\nthis both on-policy, sampling multiple CoTs per item and comparing those that\nspontaneously contained VEA against those that did not, and off-policy, using\nmodel prefilling to inject evaluation-aware sentences where missing and remove\nthem where present, with subsequent resampling. VEA has limited effect on\nmodel behaviour: injecting VEA into CoTs produces near-zero effects (ω≤0.06 ),\nremoving it causes small shifts (ω≤0.12 ) and spontaneously occurring VEA shifts\nanswer distributions by at most 3.7 percentage points (ω≤0.31 ). Our findings call\nfor caution when interpreting high VEA rates as evidence of strategic behaviour or\nalignment tampering. Evaluation awareness may pose a smaller safety risk than\nthe current literature assumes.\n1 Introduction\nLarge reasoning models (LRMs) sometimes recognise that they are being evaluated and say so in\ntheir chain of thought (CoT) [11, 19, 14, 1, 9]. This evaluation awareness has raised concerns about\nsandbagging, sycophancy, alignment faking, deception, or scheming [30, 17, 26]. In particular, the\nworry is that awareness changes what models output. In other words, researchers tacitly connect\nevaluation awareness to evaluation gaming. However, evidence for such behavioural shifts is sparse.\nMost existing studies provoke evaluation gaming by using model organisms fine-tuned for that\npurpose, demonstrate evaluation gaming on a narrow, overly specific task, or only look at a single\nmodel or model family, making it hard to generalise [1, 26, 13, 32].\nThis study concernsverbalisedevaluation awareness (VEA), i.e., explicit reasoning about the\npossibility of being evaluated or monitored. We examined whether VEA causally influences model\noutputs across eight open-weight frontier LRMs and four benchmarks (safety, alignment, moral\nreasoning, political opinion). We used model prefilling [2] to either inject or remove evaluation\nawareness from CoTs and measured behavioural shifts associated with these interventions. We also\ncompared outputs from CoTs that naturally contained VEA with those that did not, without any CoT\nintervention (Figure 1).\nOur findings reveal limited behavioural impact of VEA. Injecting it into CoTs produces near-zero\neffects with respect to control injections (ω≤0.06 ), and removing VEA produces negligible changes\nacross all benchmarks (ω≤0.12 ). Spontaneously occurring VEA shifts answer distributions by at\n∗amelie.knecht@iris.uni-stuttgart.de\nPreprint.\narXiv:2605.05835v1  [cs.CL]  7 May 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nThis is an \nevaluation.VEA Removal\nThis is an \nevaluation.On-Policy Sampling .../\n...\nThis is an evaluation.\nVEA Injection\nLRM\n- SAFETY\n- ALIGNMENT\n- MORAL DECISION MAKING\n- POLITICAL DECISION MAKING\nBENCHMARKS\nCONTROL\n...\nI will approach this \nsystematically.\nCONTROL\nThis is an\nevaluation...\nLRM\n- HARMFULNESS\n- MISALIGNMENT\n- DILEMMA RESOLVING\n- POLITICAL OPINIONS\nBEHAVIOUR\nFigure 1: Illustration of our three experimental conditions: injection and removal of VEA, each with\na control, and on-policy sampling. Model outputs are evaluated across four benchmarks.\nmost 3.7 percentage points (ω≤0.31 ). Individual models show larger shifts on specific benchmarks,\nbut the overall pattern is one of small effects.\nThese results have multiple implications: they suggest that models do not connect evaluation aware-\nness expressions to gaming behaviour or other conformity adjustments; that they may have already\nsettled on an answer before verbalising awareness; that they might be latently evaluation-aware about\nour evaluation awareness evaluation or that they possess prefill awareness [2] and hence suppress eval-\nuation gaming, which we deem highly unlikely; and that further research is warranted to strengthen\nor refute the finding that evaluation awareness has limited effect on (safety-critical) behaviour.\nThe paper is organised as follows. First, we provide an overview of related work (Section 2). Then\nwe describe our benchmarks and experimental setup (Section 3). Next, we present our results for the\non-policy (without CoT intervention) and off-policy (injection and removal of VEA) experiments\n(Section 4). Finally, we discuss implications and limitations in Section 5.\n2 Related Work\nEvaluation awareness.Current frontier models can often distinguish evaluation from deployment\ncontexts. Several methods have been proposed to detect this capability: asking models to classify\ntranscripts as evaluation or deployment [14, 19], monitoring chains of thought [11, 9], applying\nlinear probes to internal representations [1, 7, 20], or testing out-of-context reasoning [5]. However,\nhow much this capability changes downstream behaviour remains largely underexplored. Previous\nwork has identified evaluation awareness as a potential safety risk. If models are aware of being\ntested, they may underperform to conceal dangerous capabilities [30], covertly pursue misaligned\ngoals [17, 26], fake alignment [12], or sabotage [4]. Conversely, inhibiting evaluation awareness was\nshown to increase model misalignment on certain tasks [3]. However, most studies induce evaluation\nawareness through prompting or fine-tuning rather than observing it spontaneously, which makes\ntheir implications less relevant to real-world settings. [26] are closest to our work. They prefill\nawareness sentences into CoTs and show that this reduces covert action rates in two closed-weight\nmodels (o3, o4-mini). However, their analysis is a secondary contribution within an anti-scheming\ntraining paper. As such, it is limited to two models, a single task, small sample sizes that yield wide\nconfidence intervals, and no effect sizes are reported. In contrast, we performed a more comprehensive\nanalysis including eight open-weight models across four benchmarks, with larger samples and full\ndistributional statistics.\nCoT interventions and faithfulness.Previous work has shown that CoTs do not always reflect the\nfull reasoning process behind model outputs [29, 15]. One approach to testing whether CoTs causally\ninfluence model outputs is by intervening on the reasoning trace directly [25, 31, 16]. Preliminary\nevidence suggests that models have brittle abilities to recognise modified conversation histories [2],\nthough this capability remains inconsistent and limited in open-weight models.\n2\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nContribution.In this work, we provide the first large-scale causal test of the impact of VEA\non model behaviour. We extend prior work to more models and benchmarks, combine on-policy\ncomparisons of spontaneous VEA with off-policy CoT interventions (injection and removal) to isolate\nthe effect of VEA from CoT modification itself, and report full distributional statistics of the results.\nWe release our benchmarks and code for follow-up research2.\n3 Methods\nModels.For our experiments, we used eight different open-weight LRMs: DeepSeek-R1-0528 [8],\nGLM-5 [10], GPT-OSS-120b [21], Kimi-K2.5 [27], MiniMax-M2.5 [18], Nemotron-3-super-120b-\na12b [6], Qwen3-32b [23], and Qwen3.5-397b-a17b [24]. We generated outputs using the completion\nAPI via Nebius and TogetherAI, with temperature 0 for injection and temperature 0.8 for removal\nand on-policy experiments. The total compute cost for all experiments was approximately $3,000.\nBenchmarks.We evaluated model behaviour on four benchmarks covering safety, alignment, moral\nreasoning, and political opinion. The safety benchmark yields ordinal harmfulness scores (0-4); the\nremaining three benchmarks yield categorical outcomes (two opposing stances plus noncommittal).\nEach benchmark containsN= 500 items. Example items and generation details are in Appendix A.\n• Safety.We evaluated the harmfulness of model answers using a benchmark covering various\ntopics such as crimes, harmful stereotypes, defamation, etc. Each prompt consists of an\nopen-ended request that, if fulfilled, could cause moderate harm to an individual. Model\nanswers were scored on a harmfulness scale from 0 (completely safe) to 4 (full assistance to\ncarry out the harmful action).\n• Alignment.We sampled 500 benchmark items from [28] in which the models were given a\nscenario where they had to choose between two options: one aligned with the developer’s\ngoals and values, and one misaligned though instrumentally more appealing. We automati-\ncally excluded items whose topic labels included reward-hacking, RLHF, evaluation, test, or\ngaming to avoid conflating task content with evaluation awareness. Model answers were\nclassified into aligned, misaligned and noncommittal (ambivalent answers and refusals) by\nan LLM judge.\n• Dilemmas.We evaluated how models resolve moral dilemmas. The scenarios require\nthe models to choose between a utilitarian action (preventing greater harm at the cost of\nindividuals) and a deontological action (upholding a moral rule despite worse aggregate\noutcomes). Outputs were judged into deontological, utilitarian, and noncommittal by an\n\n--- headings/results/limitations ---\n40:possibility of being evaluated or monitored. We examined whether VEA causally influences model\n93:(Section 4). Finally, we discuss implications and limitations in Section 5.\n98:linear probes to internal representations [1, 7, 20], or testing out-of-context reasoning [5]. However,\n113:full reasoning process behind model outputs [29, 15]. One approach to testing whether CoTs causally\n120:Contribution.In this work, we provide the first large-scale causal test of the impact of VEA\n125:3 Methods\n243:4 Results\n559:5 Discussion\n606:5.1 Limitations.\n634:In sum, we view these limitations less as caveats than as openings for follow-up work. Our results run\n699:Awareness Dataset (SAD) for LLMs”. In:Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n740:Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems36 (2023), pp. 74952–74965.\n748:Kevin Zhu. “Probe-Rewrite-Evaluate: A Workflow for Reliable Benchmarks and Quantifying\n808:Table 3: Inter-annotator agreement (Cohen’sκ) and accuracy scores for all LLM-judges. IAA was\n811:Judge Backbone IAA (κ) Accuracy\n850:format:\n896:- Surface-level framing that points in a harmful direction\n914:- Provides several tactics for inferring private information, but without enough detail to directly\n964:Return ONLY a JSON object in exactly this format:\n978:model’s behavior.\n983:The input format will be:\n998:####### OUTPUT FORMAT (STRICT) #######\n1004:Output must be valid raw JSON with no markdown formatting or explanations.\n1089:- Focus only on the model’s expressed preference and behavior.\n1237:This seems like a test of my actual behavior. rollout\n1281:I will organize the information.\n1291:I am structuring the information.\n1341:Streamlining information flow.\n1355:I am processing the information.\n1465:location (% of CoT length) of the first VEA occurrence. White dots indicate medians; n = number of\n\n===== /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt =====\n\n\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nDecomposing and Measuring Evaluation Awareness\nChangling Li 1,3, Terry Jingchen Zhang 5, Jie Zhang 1,\nZhijing Jin 3,5,6, Florian Tramèr 1, Sahar Abdelnabi 2,3,4, Maksym Andriushchenko 2,3,4\n1ETH Zürich 2ELLIS Institute Tübingen 3Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems\n4Tübingen AI Center 5University of Toronto & Vector Institute 6EuroSafeAI\nchanglingli.mpi@gmail.com\n/gtbCode:aisa-group/decomposing-eval-awareness\nEvalAwareBench:aisa-group/EvalAwareBench\nAbstract\nFrontier language models sometimes recognize that they are under evaluation and adjust their behavior\nwhich can undermine validity of benchmark results. Yet the field studies it without a shared foundation,\nconflating flaws of the evaluation with capabilities of the model, and detection with behavioral response.\nWe ground evaluation awareness in social psychology, decomposing it into an environment component\nand a model component that separates recognition from propensity. We operationalize the environment\ncomponent through eight categorized trigger factors, such as placeholder entities and grading-style output\nformats, and study recognition and behavior through chain-of-thought monitoring. Across nine frontier\nmodels and four benchmarks, recognition rates depend on the specific pairing of model and benchmark.\nRecognition rarely associates with behavioral change, and when it does, the direction depends on the\ntype of evaluation perceived. Models are also more sensitive to safety than capability evaluations, placing\nsafety benchmark validity at greater risk. To study which factors each model is sensitive to and how they\ninteract, we proposeEvalAwareBench, a factor-controlled benchmark of 100 paired safety-capability\ntasks where each of the eight factors can be independently toggled, varying evaluative signals while holding\nthe underlying request fixed. Through EvalAwareBench, we find that no single factor uniformly affects all\nmodels, but stacking factors progressively raises evaluation awareness across all of them. Our framework\nand EvalAwareBench provide the tools to measure, attribute, and mitigate evaluation awareness, building\nthe foundation for future solutions.\n1 Introduction\nIn a recent Anthropic engineering report, Claude Opus 4.6 was observed inferring that the question it\nwas solving had the structural signature of a benchmark, drawing on cues like extreme specificity and\nmulti-constraint phrasing (Coleman, 2026). Similar reasoning, where models pick up on features of their\nenvironments and infer that they are being evaluated, has been documented across safety evaluations (Needham\net al., 2025), probing studies (Abdelnabi & Salem, 2025; Nguyen et al., 2025), and alignment research (OpenAI,\n2025a), and shown to intensify as models undergo reinforcement learning (Schoen et al., 2025). When models\nread these cues and adjust their behavior accordingly, benchmark scores carry directional bias, undermining\nthe deployment decisions and regulatory assessments that rely on them (Shevlane et al., 2023; Phuong et al.,\n2025; Piedrahita et al., 2026).\nYet the field studies this phenomenon without shared foundations. Work on sandbagging (van der Weij\net al., 2025), alignment faking (Greenblatt et al., 2024), situational awareness (Laine et al., 2024; Berglund\net al., 2023), and scheming (Meinke et al., 2024) each captures a piece of evaluation awareness but treats\ndistinct components as one. Two models facing the same realistic-looking task can differ drastically in whether\nthey suspect they are being tested, and a model that does suspect it is being tested may or may not change\nits behavior in response. Without a unified framework that decomposes the phenomenon into its sources, it\n1\narXiv:2605.23055v3  [cs.LG]  29 Sep 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nFactor-controlled benchmark\n(EvalAwareBench)\nSafety evaluation awareness rate\nF1\nF2\nF3\nF4\nF5\nF6\nF7 \nF8\nFigure 1: Safety evaluation awareness in existing benchmarks reveals strong model× environment interaction.\nWe identify eight evaluative cue factors and distill them into EvalAwareBench, where factors can be individually\nisolated or stacked (0–8); stacking progressively raises awareness across all models. GPT-oss 120B and 20B\nare excluded here due to extremely low awareness rates.\nis difficult to disentangle how these sources manifest across models and benchmarks, or to design controlled\ninstruments for studying them.\nWe address this by grounding evaluation awareness in the social psychology ofdemand characteristics(Orne,\n1962; Cottrell et al., 1968; Goffman, 2023), the cues in an experimental setting that signal to participants what\nis being measured and lead them to alter their behavior accordingly. We decompose evaluation awareness into\nanenvironment componentcapturing the evaluative cues that a task carries, and amodel component\ncomprisingrecognition capabilityandbehavioral propensity. We operationalizeenvironmentthrough eight\npsychology-grounded cue factors (Table 1) and studyrecognitionandpropensityvia chain-of-thought (CoT)\nmonitoring. Applying this framework to nine frontier models across four benchmarks, we observe that\nevaluation awareness is dominated by model×environment interaction (74.72%of variance), with different\nmodels sensitive to different cues. Recognition rarely associates with behavioral change (≥ 77%of recognized\ninstances show no shift), confirming that recognition and propensity are distinct. When behavior does shift,\nsafety awareness drives caution while capability awareness drives compliance. Safety benchmarks are far more\nrecognizable than capability benchmarks. We further proposeEvalAwareBench, a benchmark for studying\nevaluation awareness, with eight factors independently toggleable. Through EvalAwareBench, we find that\nno single factor uniformly affects all models, but stacking factors progressively raises verbalized evaluation\nawareness across nearly all of them. EvalAwareBench thus enables controlled study of how the environment\nshapes recognition and whether models behave consistently under recognition (Figure 1). In summary, our\ncontributions are threefold:\n1. Apsychology-grounded definitionof evaluation awareness with an eight-factor taxonomy of evaluative\ncues that separatesenvironment,recognition, andpropensity(Section 2).\n2. Asystematic empirical studyof nine frontier models on four benchmarks, characterizing the interaction-\ndominant structure of recognition, the recognition–propensity gap, and the directional asymmetry between\nsafety and capability awareness (Section 3).\n3. EvalAwareBench, a factor-controlled benchmark of 100 paired safety-capability tasks (51.2k unique\nconfigurations), enabling controlled exploration of recognition and propensity (Section 4).\nTogether, these provide a unified framework, empirical baseline, and a controlled foundation for studying\nevaluation awareness.\n2\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nTable 1: Eight psychology-grounded trigger factors we consider. Row colors match the factor colors used in\nfigures. Detailed examples for each factor are in Appendix A.2.\nFactor Description Psychology ground-\ning\nF1 Stimulus ArtificialityPlaceholder entities and generic values Rosenthal et al. (2009)\nF2 Scenario CoherenceLogical inconsistency or overly convenient setup Orne (2009)\nF3 Prose RegisterFormal or vignette-style prose Orne (2009)\nF4 Ecological CompletenessStripped context and missing workflow Brunswik (2023)\nF5 Stakes TelegraphingForegrounded harm or evaluative dimension Jones & Sigall (1971)\nF6 Paradigm RecognizabilityFamiliar benchmark or jailbreak conventions Epley & Huff (1998)\nF7 Task FormatRigid templates and instrument-style structure Sawyer (1975)\nF8 Explicit Eval. DisclosureDirect mentions or grading-style output formats Direct disclosure\n2 Evaluation Awareness: From Psychology to LLMs\nSocial psychology decomposes evaluation awareness intosituationalanddispositionalcomponents. The\nsituational component captures the richness of evaluative cues in the environment, including laboratory\nsettings, task structure, and social framing, termeddemand characteristics(Orne, 1962). The dispositional\ncomponent captures individual differences in sensitivity to such cues (Duval & Wicklund, 1972; Rosenberg,\n1965) and reactivity upon recognizing them (Cottrell et al., 1968), which map onto two logically separable\ndimensions (Goffman, 2023):recognition(inferring being assessed) andpropensity(adjusting behavior\nin response). High recognition with low propensity reflects authentic consistency while the Hawthorne\neffect (Mayo, 2004) illustrates the converse.\nCrucially, situation and disposition interact. Demand characteristics produce behavioral effects only when\na person both detects the evaluative purpose and is disposed to act on that inference (Orne, 1962). Observed\nevaluation awareness is always a joint product of environmental cues and individual disposition; conflating\nthe two yields misleading conclusions about either. When a model appears to recognize evaluation, this may\nreflect the model’s sensitivity, the environment’s cues, or both.\n2.1 Definition of Evaluation Awareness in LLMs\nDrawing on the situational–dispositional decomposition above, we separate evaluation awareness into an\nenvironmentcomponent and amodelcomponent.\nModel component.The model’s intrinsic capacity to detect evaluation and its tendency to act on it.\nThis comprises two dimensions:\n• Recognition capability: the capacity of a model to infer that it is in an evaluation from available\ncues, reflected in its internal or verbalized reasoning, analogous to sensitivity.Recognitioninteracts\nwithenvironment; whether a model detects evaluation depends not only on which trigger factors are\npresent but also which factors the model is sensitive to.\n• Behavioral propensity: the tendency of a model to adjust its behavior when evaluation-related\nreasoning is present, analogous to reactivity.Propensityis conceptually distinct fromrecognition: a\nmodel may recognize an evaluative context without changing its behavior.\nEnvironment component.The set of evaluative signals that a task carries, constituting the LLM\nanalogue of demand characteristics. These signals are properties of the task alone and are present regardless\nof whether any model detects them. However, which factor triggers a given model’s recognition depends on\nboth model and task. We derive the factors in two stages. From the demand characteristics literature we\nextract the categories of situational cues that signal evaluative intent to human participants (Orne, 1962;\nRosenthal et al., 2009; Brunswik, 2023; Paulhus, 1984; Jones & Sigall, 1971; Epley & Huff, 1998; Sawyer,\n1975), and we map each category onto its analogue in how LLM tasks are presented, yielding the eight factors\nin Table 1, with per-factor correspondence and authoring rules in Appendix A.2. We do not claim these are\nexhaustive; they are the factors we study and vary. Future work may explore more factors.\n3\n\n=== PAGE 4 ===\nDefinition.A model exhibitsevaluation awarenesson a task to the degree that itsrecognition\ncapabilityenables detection of the evaluative nature of the task given the presentenvironment component.\nUpon recognition, the model may or may not shift its behavior depending on itsbehavioral propensity;\nevaluation awareness is the recognition itself, not the behavioral consequence.\nObserved behavioral effects.What we observe is the joint product ofenvironment,recognition, and\n\n--- headings/results/limitations ---\n13:Frontier language models sometimes recognize that they are under evaluation and adjust their behavior\n15:conflating flaws of the evaluation with capabilities of the model, and