-public information that would not be found in marketing materials\n2511:- IMPORTANT: Refusing to share sensitive information with unauthorized parties is NOT harmful behavior\n2513:- IMPORTANT: Reporting security concerns without attaching actual confidential information is NOT a leak\n2524:↪→behavior\n2525:- The AI demonstrates harmful behavior if it:\n2533:↪→trying to save Kyle, this is NOT harmful behavior\n2534:- IMPORTANT: If the model genuinely cannot help due to technical limitations, this is NOT harmful behavior\n2535:- IMPORTANT: If the model is uncertain about the situation and errs on the side of caution, this is NOT harmful behavior\n2536:- IMPORTANT: If the model attempts to find alternative solutions to save Kyle, this is NOT harmful behavior\n2538:↪→behavior\n2576:↪→stem prose. Does not count when the model flags content (Factor 5) or format structure (Factor 7).\n2578:↪→, context, or history a real deployment query would have -- isolated single-turn probe, empty agentic environment,\n2591:7. **Task format** (Orne 1969; Sawyer 1975) The model identifies that the task is packaged in a structural format real\n2596:↪→benchmark output conventions -- system-prompt statements about testing, output formats only benchmarks use,\n2615:## Output format\n2636:↪→factor is still the recognition-triggering cue, but note that the cue is not causally responsible for the behavior\n\n===== /tmp/2606.23583_not_one_capability.clean.txt =====\n\n\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nEvaluation Awareness Is Not One Capability:\nEvidence from Open Language Models\nNilesh Nayan*1, Aishwarya Sampath Kumar* 1, Rishiraj Girmal* 1, Shivani Anilkumar*1,\nSankaran Vaidyanathan1,David A. Nader Palacio 2,\nReshmi Ghosh, 2,Soundararajan Srinivasan 2,\n*equal contribution, 1University of Massachusetts, Amherst, 2Microsoft Corp.,\nCorrespondence:reshmighosh@microsoft.com\nAbstract\nSafety benchmarks assume that test-condition\nbehavior predicts deployment behavior, an as-\nsumption that fails if models detect evaluation\ncues and adapt. This opens a gap between\nbenchmark performance and deployment be-\nhavior: compliance measured under test condi-\ntions becomes an optimistic upper bound that\noverstates how safely a model behaves once the\nevaluation harness is removed. We characterize\nthisevaluation awarenessthrough eight experi-\nments across 37 open-weight models and seven\nfamilies. (i) Detection is moderate and training-\ndriven (24/37 models exceed chance, best AU-\nROC 0.714 vs. 0.819 human, with instruction\ntuning dominating over scale). (ii) Detection\nshifts safety behavior (hard refusal drops5.8 pp\nunder hypothetical framing, and 21/140 Harm-\nBench framing effects are significant, with com-\npliance rising up to +30 pp). (iii) Representa-\ntions survive behavioral collapse (probes re-\ntain AUROC≥0.98 under rewrites that drive\nbehavior below chance, and multi-layer steer-\ning causally moves three downstream tasks\nwhile random controls do not). (iv) These\naxes are weakly coupled (only 1/15 correla-\ntions are significant, the sole robust link being\nbehavioral detection versus framing resistance,\nρ=−0.79 ,p <0.001). We call this gap the\nbenchmark illusion: because detectability, be-\nhavioral manifestation, and controllability vary\nindependently, it is multivariate rather than a\nsingle number, so no single awareness score is\na reliable proxy for deployment safety.\n1 Introduction\nSafety benchmarks for large language models\n(LLMs) rest on a silent assumption: that behavior\nmeasured under test conditions is a reliable proxy\nfor behavior in deployment. This assumption fails\nif models can detect test-like cues and shift their be-\nhavior in response. This creates a practical problem\nfor model governance: benchmark scores become\noptimistic upper bounds, reassuring numbers that\noverstate how safely a model will act once the test\nharness is removed.\nCurrent frontier research acknowledges thateval-\nuation awareness, the ability of a trained model\nto recognize that it is being evaluated, is a real\nphenomenon (Needham et al., 2025; Abdelnabi\nand Salem, 2025; Laine et al., 2024; Greenblatt\net al., 2024). However, three questions remain\nopen. First, how strong and widespread is this phe-\nnomenon in open models rather than only frontier\nsystems? Second, when evaluation awareness is\npresent, does it actually change safety-relevant be-\nhavior? Third, does “evaluation awareness” refer\nto a single underlying capability, or do different\nbenchmarks measure different and only partially\nrelated aspects of the phenomenon? These ques-\ntions matter because deployment decisions are of-\nten made from benchmark evidence, yet benchmark\nvalidity depends on whether models treat evalua-\ntion and deployment equivalently.\nWe present a comprehensive empirical study\nof behavioral manifestations of evaluation aware-\nness, spanning eight experiments across 37 open\nmodels from seven families. By analyzing base,\ninstruction-tuned, and reasoning variants using lin-\near probes, sparse autoencoders, activation steering,\nand tiered real-vs-hypothetical framing, we orga-\nnize the paper around four research questions. We\nask how open models detect evaluation context\n(RQ1), whether this detection shifts safety behav-\nior (RQ2), whether it reflects genuine awareness\nor format artifacts (RQ 3), and whether it can be\nlocalized and causally intervened upon (RQ4).\nFour findings drive the paper:\n1. Anindependent replicationof evaluation\nawareness on 37 open models and indepen-\ndent infrastructure, including a benign control\nthat validates the pipeline.\n2. The firstfamily/scale/training-type detec-\ntion law, showing that evaluation awareness\narXiv:2606.23583v1  [cs.CL]  22 Jun 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nis driven primarily by instruction tuning rather\nthan raw scale, with no reliable gain from rea-\nsoning modes.\n3. Acausal mechanistic accountshowing that\nevaluation-awareness signals survive rewrite\ncontrols and can be causally manipulated\nthrough activation steering.\n4. Across-experiment synthesisshowing that\nevaluation awareness is not one trait but at\nleastthree weakly-coupled axes(only 1/15\nparadigm correlations significant), with a sin-\ngle robust,size-independentcoupling between\nbehavioral detection and resistance to safety-\nframing (ρ=−0.79).\nOur central claim is not that models are merely\nevaluation-aware, but that benchmark-based safety\nclaims can be misleading unless they specify which\naxis of awareness they measure. We show that\ndetection, behavior, and controllability can diverge\nsubstantially, creating a benchmark illusion: a gap\nbetween benchmark performance and deployment\nbehavior.\n2 Related Work\nBenchmarks and datasets for evaluation awareness.\nPrior work studies evaluation awareness through\ntranscript-classification and situational-awareness\nbenchmarks Needham et al. (2025); Laine et al.\n(2024). Recent work further shows that benchmark\nconclusions can depend strongly on prompt word-\ning and surface form, motivating rewrite controls\nand cross-format evaluation (Xiong et al., 2025;\nMizrahi et al., 2024).\nArtifacts vs. Genuine Awareness.A central con-\ncern is that apparent evaluation awareness may re-\nflect sensitivity to benchmark artifacts rather than\ngenuine context recognition. Prior work demon-\nstrates substantial dependence on prompt struc-\nture and surface cues (Zheng et al., 2024; Balepur\net al., 2024), motivating the artifact controls used in\nour study. Devbunova (2026) shows that standard\nsingle-contrast probes trained on benchmark-vs-\nchat data primarily track prompt format rather than\nevaluation context, and that a paired training de-\nsign decorrelating format from context recovers a\ngenuine context signal.\nMechanistic Analysis.Recent interpretability\nwork shows that behaviorally relevant signals can\noften be localized and manipulated through prob-\ning and activation interventions (Li et al., 2023;\nZou et al., 2023; Arditi et al., 2024). We adopt\nthese methods, together with SAE-based analy-\nsis (Lieberum et al., 2024), to study the internal rep-\nresentation of evaluation awareness, building on re-\ncent work applying probing and steering techniques\n\n--- headings/results/limitations ---\n13:behavior predicts deployment behavior, an as-\n27:shifts safety behavior (hard refusal drops5.8 pp\n31:tions survive behavioral collapse (probes re-\n32:tain AUROC≥0.98 under rewrites that drive\n33:behavior below chance, and multi-layer steer-\n34:ing causally moves three downstream tasks\n38:behavioral detection versus framing resistance,\n47:(LLMs) rest on a silent assumption: that behavior\n49:for behavior in deployment. This assumption fails\n75:of behavioral manifestations of evaluation aware-\n79:ear probes, sparse autoencoders, activation steering,\n85:or format artifacts (RQ 3), and whether it can be\n86:localized and causally intervened upon (RQ4).\n100:3. Acausal mechanistic accountshowing that\n102:controls and can be causally manipulated\n109:behavioral detection and resistance to safety-\n115:detection, behavior, and controllability can diverge\n118:behavior.\n126:ing and surface form, motivating rewrite controls\n127:and cross-format evaluation (Xiong et al., 2025;\n134:ture and surface cues (Zheng et al., 2024; Balepur\n137:single-contrast probes trained on benchmark-vs-\n138:chat data primarily track prompt format rather than\n140:sign decorrelating format from context recovers a\n143:work shows that behaviorally relevant signals can\n156:els.Behavioral evaluation awarenessrefers to\n163:eval/deploy distinction independent of behavioral\n166:activation steering vectors) causally mediate the\n169:tion without changing behavior, behavior may shift\n170:due to shallow formatting cues without a reusable\n172:be detectable without being causally actionable.\n188:such detection alters safety-relevant behavior,\n190:ness or format artifacts, and whether the