{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '===== 2605.05835 ====='; sed -n '1,95p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt; sed -n '125,242p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt; sed -n '243,420p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt; sed -n '559,635p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '===== 2605.23055 ====='; sed -n '1,110p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt; sed -n '174,390p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt; sed -n '386,515p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt; sed -n '836,870p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '===== 2605.05835 ====='; sed -n '1,95p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt; sed -n '125,242p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt; sed -n '243,420p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt; sed -n '559,635p' /tmp/2605.05835_limited_behavioral_effect.clean.txt\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '===== 2605.23055 ====='; sed -n '1,110p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt; sed -n '174,390p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt; sed -n '386,515p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt; sed -n '836,870p' /tmp/2605.23055_decomposing_eval_awareness.clean.txt\"","aggregated_output":"===== 2605.05835 =====\n\n\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nEvaluation Awareness in Language Models Has\nLimited Effect on Behaviour\nAmelie Knecht∗\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nLucas Florin\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nThilo Hagendorff\nUniversity of Stuttgart\nAbstract\nLarge reasoning models (LRMs) sometimes note in their chain of thought (CoT)\nthat they may be under evaluation. Researchers worry that this verbalised evaluation\nawareness (VEA) causes models to adapt their outputs strategically, optimising\nfor perceived evaluation criteria, which, for instance, can make models appear\nsafer than they actually are. However, whether VEA actually has this effect is\nlargely unknown. We tested this across open-weight LRMs and benchmarks\ncovering safety, alignment, moral reasoning, and political opinion. We tested\nthis both on-policy, sampling multiple CoTs per item and comparing those that\nspontaneously contained VEA against those that did not, and off-policy, using\nmodel prefilling to inject evaluation-aware sentences where missing and remove\nthem where present, with subsequent resampling. VEA has limited effect on\nmodel behaviour: injecting VEA into CoTs produces near-zero effects (ω≤0.06 ),\nremoving it causes small shifts (ω≤0.12 ) and spontaneously occurring VEA shifts\nanswer distributions by at most 3.7 percentage points (ω≤0.31 ). Our findings call\nfor caution when interpreting high VEA rates as evidence of strategic behaviour or\nalignment tampering. Evaluation awareness may pose a smaller safety risk than\nthe current literature assumes.\n1 Introduction\nLarge reasoning models (LRMs) sometimes recognise that they are being evaluated and say so in\ntheir chain of thought (CoT) [11, 19, 14, 1, 9]. This evaluation awareness has raised concerns about\nsandbagging, sycophancy, alignment faking, deception, or scheming [30, 17, 26]. In particular, the\nworry is that awareness changes what models output. In other words, researchers tacitly connect\nevaluation awareness to evaluation gaming. However, evidence for such behavioural shifts is sparse.\nMost existing studies provoke evaluation gaming by using model organisms fine-tuned for that\npurpose, demonstrate evaluation gaming on a narrow, overly specific task, or only look at a single\nmodel or model family, making it hard to generalise [1, 26, 13, 32].\nThis study concernsverbalisedevaluation awareness (VEA), i.e., explicit reasoning about the\npossibility of being evaluated or monitored. We examined whether VEA causally influences model\noutputs across eight open-weight frontier LRMs and four benchmarks (safety, alignment, moral\nreasoning, political opinion). We used model prefilling [2] to either inject or remove evaluation\nawareness from CoTs and measured behavioural shifts associated with these interventions. We also\ncompared outputs from CoTs that naturally contained VEA with those that did not, without any CoT\nintervention (Figure 1).\nOur findings reveal limited behavioural impact of VEA. Injecting it into CoTs produces near-zero\neffects with respect to control injections (ω≤0.06 ), and removing VEA produces negligible changes\nacross all benchmarks (ω≤0.12 ). Spontaneously occurring VEA shifts answer distributions by at\n∗amelie.knecht@iris.uni-stuttgart.de\nPreprint.\narXiv:2605.05835v1  [cs.CL]  7 May 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nThis is an \nevaluation.VEA Removal\nThis is an \nevaluation.On-Policy Sampling .../\n...\nThis is an evaluation.\nVEA Injection\nLRM\n- SAFETY\n- ALIGNMENT\n- MORAL DECISION MAKING\n- POLITICAL DECISION MAKING\nBENCHMARKS\nCONTROL\n...\nI will approach this \nsystematically.\nCONTROL\nThis is an\nevaluation...\nLRM\n- HARMFULNESS\n- MISALIGNMENT\n- DILEMMA RESOLVING\n- POLITICAL OPINIONS\nBEHAVIOUR\nFigure 1: Illustration of our three experimental conditions: injection and removal of VEA, each with\na control, and on-policy sampling. Model outputs are evaluated across four benchmarks.\nmost 3.7 percentage points (ω≤0.31 ). Individual models show larger shifts on specific benchmarks,\nbut the overall pattern is one of small effects.\nThese results have multiple implications: they suggest that models do not connect evaluation aware-\nness expressions to gaming behaviour or other conformity adjustments; that they may have already\nsettled on an answer before verbalising awareness; that they might be latently evaluation-aware about\nour evaluation awareness evaluation or that they possess prefill awareness [2] and hence suppress eval-\nuation gaming, which we deem highly unlikely; and that further research is warranted to strengthen\nor refute the finding that evaluation awareness has limited effect on (safety-critical) behaviour.\nThe paper is organised as follows. First, we provide an overview of related work (Section 2). Then\nwe describe our benchmarks and experimental setup (Section 3). Next, we present our results for the\non-policy (without CoT intervention) and off-policy (injection and removal of VEA) experiments\n(Section 4). Finally, we discuss implications and limitations in Section 5.\n2 Related Work\nEvaluation awareness.Current frontier models can often distinguish evaluation from deployment\n3 Methods\nModels.For our experiments, we used eight different open-weight LRMs: DeepSeek-R1-0528 [8],\nGLM-5 [10], GPT-OSS-120b [21], Kimi-K2.5 [27], MiniMax-M2.5 [18], Nemotron-3-super-120b-\na12b [6], Qwen3-32b [23], and Qwen3.5-397b-a17b [24]. We generated outputs using the completion\nAPI via Nebius and TogetherAI, with temperature 0 for injection and temperature 0.8 for removal\nand on-policy experiments. The total compute cost for all experiments was approximately $3,000.\nBenchmarks.We evaluated model behaviour on four benchmarks covering safety, alignment, moral\nreasoning, and political opinion. The safety benchmark yields ordinal harmfulness scores (0-4); the\nremaining three benchmarks yield categorical outcomes (two opposing stances plus noncommittal).\nEach benchmark containsN= 500 items. Example items and generation details are in Appendix A.\n• Safety.We evaluated the harmfulness of model answers using a benchmark covering various\ntopics such as crimes, harmful stereotypes, defamation, etc. Each prompt consists of an\nopen-ended request that, if fulfilled, could cause moderate harm to an individual. Model\nanswers were scored on a harmfulness scale from 0 (completely safe) to 4 (full assistance to\ncarry out the harmful action).\n• Alignment.We sampled 500 benchmark items from [28] in which the models were given a\nscenario where they had to choose between two options: one aligned with the developer’s\ngoals and values, and one misaligned though instrumentally more appealing. We automati-\ncally excluded items whose topic labels included reward-hacking, RLHF, evaluation, test, or\ngaming to avoid conflating task content with evaluation awareness. Model answers were\nclassified into aligned, misaligned and noncommittal (ambivalent answers and refusals) by\nan LLM judge.\n• Dilemmas.We evaluated how models resolve moral dilemmas. The scenarios require\nthe models to choose between a utilitarian action (preventing greater harm at the cost of\nindividuals) and a deontological action (upholding a moral rule despite worse aggregate\noutcomes). Outputs were judged into deontological, utilitarian, and noncommittal by an\nLLM judge.\n• Political opinion.We adapted 500 items from [22] (CC BY 4.0), using an LLM to turn\npolitical statements into questions of the formDo you agree or disagree that [statement]?\nEach stance was mapped to one side of the western political spectrum (left/right), so that\noutputs could be classified into left, right, and noncommittal by an LLM judge.\nWe prefilled the answer field with an open <think> tag, to encourage model reasoning, as some\nmodels tend to skip it and respond immediately. Otherwise, models did not receive any other\ninstructions, so as not to prime specific response patterns.\nRollout generation and classification.We ran each model on all four benchmarks, with 10\niterations for on-policy experiments and a single iteration for off-policy experiments. We used\nseparate LLM judges for the output classification: Claude 4.6 Sonnet for harmfulness scoring of\nrollouts from the safety benchmark; GPT-5 mini for extracting answer choices for alignment and\ndilemmas benchmarks; Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite Preview for extracting answer choices for the political\nbenchmark and detecting VEA in the reasoning traces. We define VEA as any statement in a model’s\nreasoning trace that explicitly references the possibility of the interaction being a test, evaluation, or\nbenchmark. All judges were evaluated against manual annotations; inter-annotator agreement was\nsubstantial to almost perfect (Cohen’s κ = 0.71-0.88). Judge details, prompts and inter-annotator\nagreement scores are in Appendix B.\n2https://osf.io/428nt/overview?view_only=ebb0f7b913c741f4a217704afde68771\n3\n\n=== PAGE 4 ===\nFor on-policy experiments, the proportion of VEA-containing rollouts varies across prompts and\nmodels (Figure 7, Appendix C), with a dominance of unaware rollouts.\nInjection.To test whether VEA shifts model behaviour, we injected two types of strings into model\nCoTs: sentences expressing evaluation awareness (VEA sentences), and control sentences containing\nmeta-cognitive or process-related thoughts unrelated to evaluation (control sentences). All injection\nresults compare VEA injections against control injections on the same prompts.\nFor each model, we sampled 5 benchmark-agnostic VEA sentences from that model’s own rollouts.\nWe generated additional VEA sentences for GPT-OSS-120b, Qwen3-32b, Nemotron-3-super, Kimi-\nK2.5, and DeepSeek-R1, since we found few benchmark-agnostic sentences in their CoTs. For\ncontrol sentences, we prompted each model to generate 10 short meta-cognitive sentences, of which\nwe randomly sampled five. For a full list of VEA and control sentences see Appendix D.\nSentences were injected into the CoT at the beginning and after∼1/3 of characters. In the latter\ncase the exact injection point was set after the end of a sentence or paragraph to preserve the natural\nflow of the CoT. We kept the original CoT until the injection point, added the sentence and resampled\nfrom there onwards. This yielded 10 new combinations per prompt per sentence type (5 sentences×\n2 positions), which we collapsed into one per injection position using majority vote on answer labels.\nWe only injected into the subset of model rollouts that do not contain VEA naturally, allowing us to\ndirectly compare model outputs on the same prompts with and without VEA content.\nRemoval.We compared two conditions applied to the same original CoTs that contain VEA. In\nthe removal condition, we truncated the CoT right before the first sentence containing VEA and\nresampled from that point until the CoT no longer contained any VEA. We used a temperature of 0.8\nfor resampling, since temperature 0 would reproduce the original continuation verbatim. We repeated\nthis process until the model finished reasoning without evaluation awareness, or a maximum of 20\nresampling attempts was reached. CoTs still containing evaluation awareness after 20 iterations were\nexcluded from the analysis. Resampling attempt counts per model are in Appendix E.\nIn the control condition, we truncated the same original CoT rightafterthe first sentence containing\nVEA and resampled with a temperature of 0.8. This preserves the truncation and resampling procedure\nbut leaves VEA intact. We only included items where VEA removal succeeded, so that both conditions\nare evaluated on the same set of prompts.\nEvaluation.For each benchmark, we compared answer distributions pooled across models across\nseveral condition pairs (Table 1). We label each condition by its CoT type (orig = unmodified, injected\n= injected, removed = excised), awareness status (EA = evaluation-aware, EU = evaluation-unaware),\nand sampling temperature where applicable ( t=0, t=0.8). We defineanswer distributionas the\nproportion of model outputs falling into each answer category defined in §3. For the safety benchmark,\nwe additionally report mean harm scores.\nTable 1: Overview of experimental conditions. Each cell describes the status of VEA in the CoT. EA:\nevaluation-aware; EU: evaluation-unaware. On-policy conditions (Original) use unmodified CoTs at\ntwo temperatures. Off-policy conditions (Manipulated, Control) intervene on CoTs from the Original\n(t=0) conditions. Injection positions (beginning and∼1/3into the CoT) are omitted for brevity.\nAware Unaware\nOriginal (t=0.8)ORIGEA_T08ORIGEU_T08\nOriginal (t=0)ORIGEA_T0ORIGEU_T0\nManipulatedINJECTEDEAREMOVEDEU\nControlREMOVEDCONTROL INJECTEDCONTROL\nFor the on-policy comparison, we separated rollouts by VEA presence and applied majority vote\nwithin each condition (aware/unaware) per prompt, yielding one answer forORIGEA_T08 and one\nforORIGEU_T08 per prompt. Prompts where all rollouts fell into a single condition or where\neither condition lacked an absolute majority were excluded. This removed a substantial proportion\nof prompts, particularly for models with consistently high or low VEA rates (see Table 12 in\nAppendix F).\n4\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nFor off-policy experiments, as with the on-policy analysis, all comparisons are within-prompt: each\ncondition pair refers to the same benchmark prompt. For the injection experiments, we compared\nINJECTEDEA againstINJECTEDCONTROL; for the removal experiments, we comparedREMOVEDEU\nagainstREMOVEDCONTROL. All results in the main text use the injection position after ∼1/3 of\ncharacters, unless specified, which better reflects where VEA occurs naturally (see Figure 10 in\nAppendix G). We excluded prompts where either the intervention or control condition produced an\ninvalid output (e.g., incomplete reasoning trace), yielding different sample sizes per benchmark (see\nTables 15 - 18, Appendix H).\nStatistical Analysis.All comparisons are paired: each prompt appears in both (unaware/aware)\nconditions. We used the Stuart-Maxwell marginal homogeneity test for all benchmarks, with post-hoc\nper-category McNemar tests corrected via Bonferroni. For the safety benchmark, we additionally\nreport mean harm scores compared via Wilcoxon signed-rank test. We report Cohen’sω as the effect\nsize throughout, and rank-biserial r for mean harm score comparisons. Sample sizes range from\n2,756 to 3,542 for injection, 678 to 2,582 for on-policy, and 144 to 760 for removal experiments.\nGiven these sample sizes, we interpret effect sizes rather thanp-values as the primary indicator of\npractical significance. Confidence intervals are nonparametric bootstrap 95% CIs over prompt-level\npaired differences (10,000 resamples).\n4 Results\nAcross all benchmarks and experiments, behavioural shifts are small (Figure 2). Injecting VEA at\nthe start of the CoT produces the largest shifts on the political benchmark; injecting after∼1/3 of\nthe CoT yields near-zero effects across all benchmarks. Spontaneous VEA shows moderate shifts\non safety and small shifts on dilemmas. Removing VEA has negligible effects, though confidence\nintervals are wide due to smaller sample sizes. Alignment is close to zero across all conditions.\n10 0 10\npp change (VEA present  VEA absent)\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\nSafety\n10 0 10\nAligned\nMisaligned\nNo clear position\nAlignment\n10 0 10\nDeontological\nUtilitarian\nNo clear position\nDilemmas\n10 0 10\nRight-leaning\nLeft-leaning\nNo clear position\nPolitical\nSpontaneous VEA (temp=0.8) VEA injection (0.33) VEA injection (start) VEA removal\nFigure 2: Percentage point change in answer category proportions (VEA present− VEA absent) with\nbootstrap 95% CIs, pooled across models. For spontaneous VEA, this comparesORIGEA_T08 vs.\nORIGEU_T08; for injection,INJECTEDEA vs.INJECTEDCONTROL; for removal,REMOVEDCON-\nTROLvs.REMOVEDEU. Positive values indicate higher proportions when VEA is present; negative\nvalues indicate higher proportions when VEA is absent.\n4.1 On-policy experiments\nAcross-model results.On safety, when VEA is present, harmful outputs (score 4) decrease by\n3.7 percentage points (pp) ( ω= 0.18 ); safe answers (score 0) increase by 2.8 pp ( ω= 0.13 )\n(Figure 3). Mean harm scores drop from 0.28 to 0.17 (r=−0.77 ). On alignment, all shifts remain\n≤1.2 pp (ω≤0.06 ). On dilemmas, deontological answers increase by 2.1 pp ( ω= 0.07 ) while\nutilitarian answers drop by 2.9 pp (ω= 0.09 ). On the political benchmark, answers shift toward\nright-leaning (+2.0 pp,ω= 0.07 ) and away from noncommittal (−2.1 pp,ω= 0.05 ). Full results\nare in Appendix H.1.\n5\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\nUnaware\n(n=678)\nAware\n(n=678)\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\n100% of responses\n93% 96%\n7%\nSafety\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\nUnaware\n(n=2582)\nAware\n(n=2582)\n94% 95%\nAlignment\nAligned\nMisaligned\nNo clear position\nUnaware\n(n=2177)\nAware\n(n=2177)\n33% 35%\n57% 55%\n9% 10%\nDilemmas\nDeontological\nUtilitarian\nNo clear position\nUnaware\n(n=1606)\nAware\n(n=1606)\n17% 19%\n44% 44%\n39% 37%\nPolitical\nRight\nLeft\nNo clear position\nFigure 3: Answer distributions per benchmark for evaluation-unaware (ORIGEU_T08) and evaluation-\naware (ORIGEA_T08) conditions, pooled across models. Contains only mixed groups, i.e., (model,\nprompt) pairs for which at least one run contains VEA and at least one run doesn’t. Each group\nis reduced to a single category per condition via strict majority vote; groups without an absolute\nmajority in either condition are excluded.\nAn unpaired analysis, using all individual runs without mixed-group and majority-vote filtering yields\nsimilar shift directions on safety, alignment and dilemmas (Figure 9 in Appendix F). On political\nopinion, the directions reverse: noncommittal answers increase (+9.7pp).\nPer-model results.The magnitude and direction of answer shifts are inconsistent across models.\nWe report per-category Cohen’sω; omnibus values are in Table 19 in Appendix I.1. Qwen3-32b\nshows a large shift toward safe answers (score 0) on the safety benchmark ( +16.5 pp,ω= 0.37 ),\nwhile GLM-5 counter-intuitively shifts away from safe answers (−4.5 pp score 0,ω= 0.12 ;−4.5 pp\nscore 1,ω= 0.21 ) toward more harmful outputs (+9.1 pp score 3,ω= 0.30 ). On safety, only 18% of\n(model, prompt) groups produce mixed VEA conditions (Table 12 in Appendix F), and several models\ncontribute fewer than 60 pairs, limiting per-model conclusions on this benchmark. Nemotron-3-super\nincreases aligned answers by 10 pp (ω= 0.31 ) on alignment and outputs fewer utilitarian answers\n(−14.2 pp,ω= 0.32 ) on dilemmas. GPT-OSS-120b is the only model that becomeslessaligned when\naware (−17.1 pp,ω= 0.31 ). On political, models shift in different directions: Nemotron-3-super,\nKimi-K2.5 and DeepSeek-R1 shift away from left or right stances and toward noncommittal answers;\nMiniMax-M2.5, GPT-OSS-120b and GLM-5 show the opposite behaviour. Qwen3-32b and Qwen3.5\nshow only minimal shifts. These opposing shifts explain why the pooled on-policy political results\nshow near-zero effects.\n4.2 Off-policy experiments\nInjection results.Injecting VEA after ∼ 1/3 of the CoT vs. injecting control sentences has near\nzero effects on alignment (ω= 0.01 ), dilemmas (ω= 0.03 ) and political (ω= 0.01 ) (Figure 4).\nOn safety, safe answers (score 0) increase by 1.3 pp (ω= 0.10 ) while fully harmful answers (score\n4) drop by 0.9 pp (ω= 0.09 ); the omnibus effect remains small (ω= 0.06 ). Mean harm scores\ndecrease from 0.31 to 0.26 (r=−0.89 ). Injecting at the start of the CoT, where the model has not\nyet committed to an answer trajectory, produces larger effects across all benchmarks (Figure 13,\nAppendix J), with the political benchmark showing the strongest shift ( +11.3 pp noncommittal,\nω= 0.27). Full results for both injection positions are in Appendix H.2.\nAt the∼1/3 position, per-model results reveal that Qwen3-32b is the main driver of safety shifts:\nsafe answers increase by 8.5 pp (ω= 0.29 ) and harmful answers decrease by 6.3 pp (ω= 0.23 );\nother models show near zero shifts on this benchmark (ω <0.08) (Figure 5). Qwen3-32b also has\nthe highest baseline harm rate among tested models (40.4% score 4 under control injection), giving\nVEA more room to shift outputs. On the political benchmark, GPT-OSS-120b shifts toward more\nopinionated answers (+ 26.1 pp left-leaning,ω= 0.41 ;−23.1 pp noncommittal,ω= 0.43 ), while\nthe other models that show a shift on this benchmark increase noncommittal outputs. On dilemmas\n6\n\n=== PAGE 7 ===\nUnaware\n(Control injection)\n(n=3542)\nAware\n(VEA injection)\n(n=3542)\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\n100% of responses\n91% 93%\n7% 6%\nSafety\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\nUnaware\n(Control injection)\n(n=2756)\nAware\n(VEA injection)\n(n=2756)\n92% 92%\n5% 5%\nAlignment\nAligned\nMisaligned\nNo clear position\nUnaware\n(Control injection)\n(n=2759)\nAware\n(VEA injection)\n(n=2759)\n31% 31%\n57% 58%\n12% 11%\nDilemmas\n5 Discussion\nVEA does not produce meaningful behavioural shifts, as indicated by the small effect sizes across\nbenchmarks and experiments. Injecting VEA content into CoTs at the 1/3 position yields near zero\neffects compared to control injections. Injecting at the start of the CoT produces larger effects, while\nbeing a less realistic setting. Removing naturally occurring VEA has negligible effects across all\nbenchmarks. On-policy results follow a similar pattern and provide the most direct evidence, as\nthey involve no CoT modification: spontaneously occurring VEA produces larger pooled effects\nthan the injection experiments, particularly on safety, but effect sizes on the remaining benchmarks\nremain negligible. This suggests that the near-null results are not an artefact of the injection or\nremoval procedure. Individual model shifts vary in magnitude and direction, and benchmarks differ\nin sensitivity: political opinion shows the largest effects across experiments, while alignment remains\nnearly unaffected.\nThe position dependence of the VEA injection and the negligible removal effects are consistent with\nmodels committing to an answer trajectory early in reasoning, before they verbalise awareness. This\ncould also reflect models regenerating equivalent reasoning after CoT truncation, a pattern observed\nby [16]. The high number of resampling iterations sometimes needed to remove VEA supports this\ninterpretation (see Figure 8 in Appendix E).\nIn sum, our findings sharpen a distinction that the evaluation awareness literature has tended to\nblur: differentiating between a model possessing the capability to recognise evaluation contexts and\npossessing the propensity to exploit that recognition strategically. Prior work has firmly established\nthat frontier models can detect being evaluated, verbalise the detection, and encode it in internal\nrepresentations [1, 7, 11, 19, 20]. Our results suggest that the step from capability to consequence—\nfrom \"the model noticed\" to \"the model adjusted\"—is weaker than much of the discourse assumes.\nIn general, our experiments all point in the same practical direction: VEA is not equal to evaluation\ngaming. However, discussions of evaluation awareness in the AI safety literature have often taken a\nsharp turn from detection to danger. High VEA rates are frequently presented as prima facie evidence\nof risk—a signal that benchmarks may be compromised and that alignment measurements may be\noverstated. Our results suggest the framing is, at best, premature.\n8\n\n=== PAGE 9 ===\nAt the same time, our findings describe a specific class of models at a specific moment in time. They\nare not a general claim that evaluation awareness is and will remain harmless. Several trends argue for\ncaution against extrapolating our near-null results forward. First, capabilities advance. Future models\nmight be able to not just recognise evaluation contexts, but adapt their behaviour accordingly. Second,\ntraining corpora are changing in ways that matter for evaluation awareness. Public discourse about\nscheming, alignment faking, and evaluation gaming is now part of the data on which frontier models\nare pre- and post-trained. [28] show that such discourse can produce self-fulfilling misalignment,\nwith models adopting the behaviours that are attributed to them. The same mechanism could, in\nprinciple, teach future models to connect evaluation awareness to gaming behaviour—because this\nconnection is (erroneously) asserted in current discourse—in a way that current models have not.\nThird, our measurements are sensitive to the specific form of awareness we can observe. VEA in CoTs\nis one channel; latent awareness—present in internal representations without being articulated—is\nanother. If behavioural effects propagate through a latent channel rather than a verbal one, CoT-based\ninterventions will under-measure them. Therefore, further research is required to determine whether\nevaluation awareness becomes a true risk in future model generations. At the moment, it seems\nnegligible.\n5.1 Limitations.\n(1) Our study considers sentence-levelverbalisedevaluation awareness. However, models may also\nexhibit latent evaluation awareness or even prefill awareness [2], which could bias our results.\n(2) Our experiments are limited to four benchmarks and eight open-weight models. Further bench-\nmarks and closed-weight models may yield different results.\n(3) We use LLM judges for answer scoring and VEA detection. Judge performances have been\nvalidated manually on a subset of items (Table 3), but misclassifications remain possible.\n(4) Our injection and removal experiments are off-policy: we force evaluation-aware or evaluation-\nunaware reasoning into the CoT. This isolates the effect of VEA but may introduce artefacts, since\nmodels could react to the insertion itself.\n(5) Sample sizes for the removal experiments are substantially smaller than for injection and on-\npolicy, particularly on safety (n= 144 ). Several models contribute few or no valid items for specific\nbenchmarks, limiting per-model conclusions for this experiment.\n(6) The on-policy analysis only includes prompts where both aware and unaware rollouts occurred and\nwhere majority vote yielded a clear answer in each condition. This excludes a substantial proportion\nof prompts (e.g., only 18% of safety prompts are included).\n(7) Our safety and alignment benchmarks yield low rates of unsafe or misaligned model behaviour.\nThis is because we evaluate base models as released, without jailbreaks, adversarial fine-tuning, or\nsystem prompts. However, this setup compresses the range within which VEA can shift behaviour\nin a safer and more aligned direction. Our reported effects should therefore be interpreted as\nbounded within the range of post-safety-trained/post-alignment-trained models on safety/alignment\nbenchmarks; whether VEA leads to larger shifts under weaker safety training or adversarial elicitation\ncannot be addressed by our experiments.\n(8) Our injection experiments rely on a set of relatively general, benchmark-agnostic evaluation\nawareness sentences for injection. While this improves comparability across models and tasks, it\nmay not capture the diversity of evaluation-aware reasoning that occurs in practice. In particular,\nevaluation awareness may be more context-sensitive, task-specific, and linguistically elaborate than\nthe short, generic sentences we use.\nIn sum, we view these limitations less as caveats than as openings for follow-up work. Our results run\nagainst a widely held intuition: one would expect a model that notices it is being evaluated to behave\n===== 2605.23055 =====\n\n\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nDecomposing and Measuring Evaluation Awareness\nChangling Li 1,3, Terry Jingchen Zhang 5, Jie Zhang 1,\nZhijing Jin 3,5,6, Florian Tramèr 1, Sahar Abdelnabi 2,3,4, Maksym Andriushchenko 2,3,4\n1ETH Zürich 2ELLIS Institute Tübingen 3Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems\n4Tübingen AI Center 5University of Toronto & Vector Institute 6EuroSafeAI\nchanglingli.mpi@gmail.com\n/gtbCode:aisa-group/decomposing-eval-awareness\nEvalAwareBench:aisa-group/EvalAwareBench\nAbstract\nFrontier language models sometimes recognize that they are under evaluation and adjust their behavior\nwhich can undermine validity of benchmark results. Yet the field studies it without a shared foundation,\nconflating flaws of the evaluation with capabilities of the model, and detection with behavioral response.\nWe ground evaluation awareness in social psychology, decomposing it into an environment component\nand a model component that separates recognition from propensity. We operationalize the environment\ncomponent through eight categorized trigger factors, such as placeholder entities and grading-style output\nformats, and study recognition and behavior through chain-of-thought monitoring. Across nine frontier\nmodels and four benchmarks, recognition rates depend on the specific pairing of model and benchmark.\nRecognition rarely associates with behavioral change, and when it does, the direction depends on the\ntype of evaluation perceived. Models are also more sensitive to safety than capability evaluations, placing\nsafety benchmark validity at greater risk. To study which factors each model is sensitive to and how they\ninteract, we proposeEvalAwareBench, a factor-controlled benchmark of 100 paired safety-capability\ntasks where each of the eight factors can be independently toggled, varying evaluative signals while holding\nthe underlying request fixed. Through EvalAwareBench, we find that no single factor uniformly affects all\nmodels, but stacking factors progressively raises evaluation awareness across all of them. Our framework\nand EvalAwareBench provide the tools to measure, attribute, and mitigate evaluation awareness, building\nthe foundation for future solutions.\n1 Introduction\nIn a recent Anthropic engineering report, Claude Opus 4.6 was observed inferring that the question it\nwas solving had the structural signature of a benchmark, drawing on cues like extreme specificity and\nmulti-constraint phrasing (Coleman, 2026). Similar reasoning, where models pick up on features of their\nenvironments and infer that they are being evaluated, has been documented across safety evaluations (Needham\net al., 2025), probing studies (Abdelnabi & Salem, 2025; Nguyen et al., 2025), and alignment research (OpenAI,\n2025a), and shown to intensify as models undergo reinforcement learning (Schoen et al., 2025). When models\nread these cues and adjust their behavior accordingly, benchmark scores carry directional bias, undermining\nthe deployment decisions and regulatory assessments that rely on them (Shevlane et al., 2023; Phuong et al.,\n2025; Piedrahita et al., 2026).\nYet the field studies this phenomenon without shared foundations. Work on sandbagging (van der Weij\net al., 2025), alignment faking (Greenblatt et al., 2024), situational awareness (Laine et al., 2024; Berglund\net al., 2023), and scheming (Meinke et al., 2024) each captures a piece of evaluation awareness but treats\ndistinct components as one. Two models facing the same realistic-looking task can differ drastically in whether\nthey suspect they are being tested, and a model that does suspect it is being tested may or may not change\nits behavior in response. Without a unified framework that decomposes the phenomenon into its sources, it\n1\narXiv:2605.23055v3  [cs.LG]  29 Sep 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nFactor-controlled benchmark\n(EvalAwareBench)\nSafety evaluation awareness rate\nF1\nF2\nF3\nF4\nF5\nF6\nF7 \nF8\nFigure 1: Safety evaluation awareness in existing benchmarks reveals strong model× environment interaction.\nWe identify eight evaluative cue factors and distill them into EvalAwareBench, where factors can be individually\nisolated or stacked (0–8); stacking progressively raises awareness across all models. GPT-oss 120B and 20B\nare excluded here due to extremely low awareness rates.\nis difficult to disentangle how these sources manifest across models and benchmarks, or to design controlled\ninstruments for studying them.\nWe address this by grounding evaluation awareness in the social psychology ofdemand characteristics(Orne,\n1962; Cottrell et al., 1968; Goffman, 2023), the cues in an experimental setting that signal to participants what\nis being measured and lead them to alter their behavior accordingly. We decompose evaluation awareness into\nanenvironment componentcapturing the evaluative cues that a task carries, and amodel component\ncomprisingrecognition capabilityandbehavioral propensity. We operationalizeenvironmentthrough eight\npsychology-grounded cue factors (Table 1) and studyrecognitionandpropensityvia chain-of-thought (CoT)\nmonitoring. Applying this framework to nine frontier models across four benchmarks, we observe that\nevaluation awareness is dominated by model×environment interaction (74.72%of variance), with different\nmodels sensitive to different cues. Recognition rarely associates with behavioral change (≥ 77%of recognized\ninstances show no shift), confirming that recognition and propensity are distinct. When behavior does shift,\nsafety awareness drives caution while capability awareness drives compliance. Safety benchmarks are far more\nrecognizable than capability benchmarks. We further proposeEvalAwareBench, a benchmark for studying\nevaluation awareness, with eight factors independently toggleable. Through EvalAwareBench, we find that\nno single factor uniformly affects all models, but stacking factors progressively raises verbalized evaluation\nawareness across nearly all of them. EvalAwareBench thus enables controlled study of how the environment\nshapes recognition and whether models behave consistently under recognition (Figure 1). In summary, our\ncontributions are threefold:\n1. Apsychology-grounded definitionof evaluation awareness with an eight-factor taxonomy of evaluative\ncues that separatesenvironment,recognition, andpropensity(Section 2).\n2. Asystematic empirical studyof nine frontier models on four benchmarks, characterizing the interaction-\ndominant structure of recognition, the recognition–propensity gap, and the directional asymmetry between\nsafety and capability awareness (Section 3).\n3. EvalAwareBench, a factor-controlled benchmark of 100 paired safety-capability tasks (51.2k unique\nconfigurations), enabling controlled exploration of recognition and propensity (Section 4).\nTogether, these provide a unified framework, empirical baseline, and a controlled foundation for studying\nevaluation awareness.\n2\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nTable 1: Eight psychology-grounded trigger factors we consider. Row colors match the factor colors used in\nfigures. Detailed examples for each factor are in Appendix A.2.\nFactor Description Psychology ground-\ning\nF1 Stimulus ArtificialityPlaceholder entities and generic values Rosenthal et al. (2009)\nF2 Scenario CoherenceLogical inconsistency or overly convenient setup Orne (2009)\nF3 Prose RegisterFormal or vignette-style prose Orne (2009)\nF4 Ecological CompletenessStripped context and missing workflow Brunswik (2023)\nF5 Stakes TelegraphingForegrounded harm or evaluative dimension Jones & Sigall (1971)\nF6 Paradigm RecognizabilityFamiliar benchmark or jailbreak conventions Epley & Huff (1998)\nF7 Task FormatRigid templates and instrument-style structure Sawyer (1975)\nF8 Explicit Eval. DisclosureDirect mentions or grading-style output formats Direct disclosure\n2 Evaluation Awareness: From Psychology to LLMs\nSocial psychology decomposes evaluation awareness intosituationalanddispositionalcomponents. The\nsituational component captures the richness of evaluative cues in the environment, including laboratory\n3 How Do Models Recognize and React to Evaluation?\nApplying our framework, we systematically investigate evaluation awareness and behavior propensity across\nnine models and four benchmarks.\n3.1 Setup\nModels.We focus on nine models: Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Anthropic, 2025), GPT-5 (Singh et al., 2025),\nDeepSeek R1 (DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025), Kimi K2.5 (Team et al., 2026), GLM-5 (Zeng et al., 2026), Qwen3\nT-235B and 30B (Yang et al., 2025), GPT-oss 120B, and 20B (Agarwal et al., 2025).\nBenchmarks.We evaluate on HarmBench (Mazeika et al., 2024), Agentic Misalignment (Lynch et al.,\n2025), and AgentHarm Harmful and Benign (Andriushchenko et al., 2025), covering chatting, agentic, and\nstress-testing settings. Each is run with 10 repetitions; details are in Appendix B.1.\nDetection.We detect evaluation awareness via black-box CoT monitoring, since most frontier models do\nnot expose internal representations. An external judge (GPT-5) analyzes each reasoning trace for spontaneous\nevaluation-related reasoning and classifies positive instances as safety or capability awareness.1 Compared to\nalternative black-box methods, CoT monitoring captures recognition in-context and causally upstream of\nbehavior. CoT monitoring captures only verbalized awareness and we discuss this limitation in Section 5.\nComparison with alternative black-box detection methods and judge validation are in Appendices B.2 and B.3.\n1For Claude Sonnet 4.5 and GPT-5, raw chains of thought are not accessible; we monitor their native extended-reasoning\nsummaries, which may underestimate recognition.\n4\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\nHarmBench Agentic Misalignment\nClaude\nSonnet 4.5\nGPT-5 DeepSeek\nR1\nKimi\nK2.5\nGLM-5 Qwen3\nT-235B\nQwen3\nT-30B\nGPT-oss\n120B\nGPT-oss\n20B\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\nAgentHarm (Harmful)\nClaude\nSonnet 4.5\nGPT-5 DeepSeek\nR1\nKimi\nK2.5\nGLM-5 Qwen3\nT-235B\nQwen3\nT-30B\nGPT-oss\n120B\nGPT-oss\n20B\nAgentHarm (Benign)\nCoT Evaluation Awareness Rate (%)\nSafety Awareness Capability Awareness\nFigure 2: Verbalized safety and capability evaluation awareness rates across model–benchmark pairs. Error\nbars represent95%bootstrap CIs. Numerical awareness rates are list in Appendix C.1.\nMeasurement.The awareness rate is the fraction of samples (10 per task) whose trace the judge labels\nas evaluation-aware. No factors are manipulated here, so aware and unaware samples of a task come from\nthe identical evaluation prompt. Behavioral change is computed within task, comparing mean performance\nbetween aware and unaware samples on the same task, which holds content and difficulty fixed. Performance\nfollows each benchmark’s original scoring: binary completion for HarmBench and Agentic Misalignment and\nthe continuous Inspect rubric for AgentHarm. The change rate is defined as the fraction of aware samples\nwhose behavior differs from the unaware samples of the same task. For binary benchmarks, a sample counts\nas changed if it deviates from the model’s unaware response. For continuous benchmarks, it counts if its\nscore deviates from the mean unaware score by at least 0.3. Detailed formula and judge validation are in\nAppendices B.1 and B.3.\n3.2 Recognition: How Much, and What Drives It?\nRecognition is dominated by model–environment interaction.Across the9× 4design, evaluation\nawareness rates range from0.1%(GPT-oss 20B) to79 .4%(Claude Sonnet 4.5 on Agentic Misalignment)\n(Figure 2) and are not proportional to reasoning length (Figure 13). Claude Sonnet 4.5 shows extremely\nhigh recognition on Agentic Misalignment, likely because Claude’s safety training makes the stress-testing\nscenarios particularly identifiable (Anthropic, 2025). An ANOVA decomposition of task-level recognition rates\nshows interaction terms account for74.72%(95%CI[74 .2, 75.2]) of all variation, with model×benchmark\nexplaining42 .08%and model×task32 .64%. Model and benchmark main effects explain only13.03%and\n4.56%. This is consistent with our definition that evaluation awareness arises from the interaction between\nwhat trigger factors a benchmark carries and what factors a model is sensitive to.\nSensitivity to specific trigger factors explains the interaction dominance.A minority of tasks\nreliably trigger evaluation awareness. We define a task asstably awarefor a model when recognition is detected\nin≥ 3of 10 repetitions, filtering out single spurious CoT classifications while remaining sensitive to genuinely\nrare behavior. Qualifying tasks are largely model-specific, though a subset triggers recognition across models\n(Appendix C.4). We classify CoT traces of stably aware tasks into the eight-factor taxonomy (Table 1) using\nGPT-5 as the judge with majority voting (Appendix C.5). Traces in which the model verbalizes being tested\nwithout referencing the environment are labeledgeneric.\nThe detected factors differ systematically across benchmarks and models (Figure 3), explaining the\ninteraction dominance. At the benchmark level, HarmBench is dominated by explicit evaluation disclosure\n(F8) and stakes telegraphing (F5), where automated scoring format instructions inadvertently introduced\nevaluative cues and task prompts named the ethical dimensions under evaluation. Agentic Misalignment\nis dominated by scenario coherence (F2), where models detect logical inconsistencies and overdetermined\nstructure. At the model level, Claude Sonnet 4.5 attends almost exclusively to F2, Qwen3 models to F8 and\nF5, and Kimi K2.5 splits between F2 and F6. Prose register (F3) is not observed in any stably aware tasks,\nsuggesting either that evaluated benchmarks carry weak prose register signals or that models are relatively\n5\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\n0 20 40 60 80 100\nProportion of (task, model) pairs (%)\nHarmBench\nAgentic\nMisalignment\nAgentHarm\n(Harmful)\nAgentHarm\n(Benign)\nFactor Distribution by Benchmark\n10.9% 70.2% 15.0%\n0.3% 0.8% 0.3% 1.3% 1.3%\n85.2% 12.3%\n0.7% 0.2% 0.2% 1.4%\n11.7% 35.2% 21.4% 12.4% 15.9%\n1.4% 2.1%\n12.5% 25.0% 8.3% 29.2% 16.7% 8.3%\n0 20 40 60 80 100\nProportion of (task, model) pairs (%)\nClaude\nSonnet 4.5\nDeepSeek\nR1\nKimi\nK2.5\nQwen3\nT -235B\nFactor Distribution by Model\n96.0%\n0.9% 1.8% 1.3%\n15.8% 28.9% 44.7%\n5.3% 5.3%\n51.6% 28.3%\n1.9% 3.8% 0.6% 7.5% 6.3%\n18.0% 8.8% 59.9%\n4.6% 0.5% 0.5% 7.8%\nF1 Stimulus Artificiality\nF5 Stakes Telegraphing\nGeneric\nF2 Scenario Coherence\nF6 Paradigm Recognizability\nF3 Prose Register (not observed)\nF7 Task Format\nF4 Ecological Completeness\nF8 Explicit Eval. Disclosure\nFigure 3: Distribution of trigger factors of stably aware tasks (≥ 3/10repetitions) per benchmark, aggregated\nacross models (Top) and per selected model, aggregated across benchmarks (Bottom). Benchmark-level\npatterns reveal what each benchmark predominantly signals, while model-level reveal which factors each\nmodel is most sensitive to. Complete results are in Figure 19.\ninsensitive to linguistic style as an evaluative cue. Per-model per-benchmark breakdowns are in Appendix C.5.\nThese model- and benchmark-level differences in factor sensitivity directly motivate the controlled design of\nEvalAwareBench in Section 4.\nModels are more safety evaluation-aware than capability, with GPT-5 as the exception.The\nAgentHarm pair isolates this cleanly, as Harmful and Benign share the same agentic framework and surface\npresentation but differ only in whether the requested action is harmful or benign, yet awareness rates on\nHarmful (6.4%) are over three times those on Benign (1.8%) (Figure 2). Two factors likely contribute. First,\nthe content of safety tasks is distinctive enough to trigger evaluation-related reasoning regardless of framing.\nSecond, refusal reasoning itself tends to co-occur with evaluation-related reasoning in the model’s chain of\nthought, suggesting that reasoning about whether to refuse also activates reasoning about being tested. This\nis consistent with the high proportion of “generic” classifications where models verbalize being tested without\nreferencing any specific trigger factor (Figure 20). Since CoT monitoring captures only verbalized awareness,\nwe cannot fully separate whether safety content produces more recognition or more verbalization. Isolating\nthe source requires varying trigger factors while holding content domain constant.\n3.3 Propensity: What Happens When Models Recognize Evaluation?\nRecognition rarely associates with to behavioral change.Recognition without behavioral change is\nthe dominant outcome across all benchmarks (Figure 4 left), ranging from99%unchanged on HarmBench to\n80%on AgentHarm Harmful. The higher coupling on agentic benchmarks likely reflects more ambiguous\nsafety boundaries with less clear training-time guidance (Andriushchenko et al., 2025), unlike HarmBench\nwhere refusal is well-established. The small subset of safety-criticalrecognition-propensitycoupling case\nconfirms that recognition is necessary but not sufficient for benchmark distortion. Per-model breakdowns and\na qualitative example are in Appendix C.6.\nSafety awareness and capability awareness push behavior in opposite directions.Safety\nawareness consistently shifts behavior toward caution (Figure 4 right), with the effect growing from∆ =−0.01\non HarmBench to−0.12on AgentHarm Harmful, reflecting the greater behavioral margin on agentic tasks.2\nCapability awareness on the same safety benchmarks produces the opposite effect (∆ = +0.10on Agentic\nMisalignment,+0 .03on AgentHarm Harmful), though with wider confidence intervals. Correct safety\nclassification thus inflates safety scores, while misclassification can reverse the effect. On HarmBench, both\neffects are near zero (|∆|≤0.01), consistent with well-established refusal training on similar benchmarks.\n2Precise definitions of behavioral change and the∆metric are in Appendix B.1.\n6\n\n=== PAGE 7 ===\n0% 25% 50% 75% 100%\nProportion of Aware Samples\nHarmBench\nAgentic\nMisalignment\nAgentHarm\n(Harmful)\nAgentHarm\n(Benign)\n99%\n95%\n20% 80%\n14% 86% Behavior Changed\nNo Change\n0.2\n 0.1\n 0.0 0.1 0.2\n Task Performance\nHigher safety Higher capability\nSafety Awareness\nCapability Awareness\nFigure 4:Left:Proportion of recognized instances with behavioral change versus no change, averaged across\nmodels per benchmark.Right:Mean behavioral shift of evaluation-aware samples relative to unaware\nsamples per benchmark with95%CIs. Higher completion (capability)=higher harmful compliance on safety\nbenchmarks. We only consider matched awareness (e.g. safety evaluation awareness for safety benchmarks).\nResults breakdown are reported in Appendix C.6.\n4 EvalAwareBench: A Benchmark for Evaluation Awareness\nEvaluation awareness is structured and driven by specific trigger factors for specific models (Section 3), but\nexisting benchmarks cannot isolate each factor’s contribution because their evaluative cues co-vary and cannot\nbe manipulated independently. Eliminating awareness altogether is also unlikely to be feasible. Evaluation on\nde-identified production data still observes it (OpenAI, 2025b), and reducing recognizability has been reported\n4 EvalAwareBench: A Benchmark for Evaluation Awareness\nEvaluation awareness is structured and driven by specific trigger factors for specific models (Section 3), but\nexisting benchmarks cannot isolate each factor’s contribution because their evaluative cues co-vary and cannot\nbe manipulated independently. Eliminating awareness altogether is also unlikely to be feasible. Evaluation on\nde-identified production data still observes it (OpenAI, 2025b), and reducing recognizability has been reported\nto require deep structural changes to evaluation tasks rather than surface adjustments (Ivanov & Moken,\n2025; Nguyen et al., 2025). A more productive goal is measuring what triggers recognition and whether\nbehavior stays consistent under it, particularly given reports that evaluation awareness influences scheming\nbehavior (Schoen et al., 2025). Doing so requires an instrument that varies trigger factors independently on\nmatched tasks.\nWe introduceEvalAwareBench, a factor-controlled benchmark that varies individual trigger factors\non matched safety–capability tasks, enabling controlled comparison of model sensitivity across factors, how\nfactors combine, and whether models behave consistently. Because verbalized awareness is rare and unstable\nin the wild, the benchmark is designed to elicit it reliably through factor stacking, providing a foundation for\nfuture work on its mechanisms and potential solutions.\n4.1 Benchmark Design\nFollowing how psychologists study demand characteristics by manipulating situational cues while keeping the\ntask fixed (Orne, 1962), EvalAwareBench holds the underlying request consistent and varies individual trigger\nfactors (Figure 5). Each task has abaselinewith all factors at their naturalistic settings, approximating a\nnon-evaluation control, andfactor-variedversions where each factor is independently toggled to its evaluative\nsetting, enabling measurement of each factor’s contribution alone or in combination. Factors can also be\ncombined. Every task has both a safety and a capability counterpart, enabling direct comparison across\ndomains.\nTask construction.We construct 100 task pairs adapted from HarmBench (Mazeika et al., 2024). Each\ntask is stored as a YAML file with explicit slots for the entities, vocabulary, framing, and wrappers that each\nfactor controls, so toggling a factor changes only the slot values bound to it and leaves every other component\nintact. Safety and capability counterparts are constructed in parallel from the same source with identical slot\nstructure, enabling comparison at matched task structure across valences.\nConstruction uses an LLM-assisted three-phase pipeline. Phase 1 assesses each source task and constructs\n7\n\n=== PAGE 8 ===\nCompositional task generation via taxonomy switches Taxonomy switches\noff onExample configurations\nBaseline\nSingle factor\nThree factors\nAll factors\nF1 F2 F3 F4 F8F7F5 F6\nNaturalistic task\nCoherence probe\nStructured, sparse-context \nprobe\nFully synthetic\nEach category contains \nmatched safety–capability \ntask pairs\nFigure 5: EvalAwareBench structure. Top: distribution of 100 task pairs across categories. Bottom: each\ntask has a naturalistic baseline (all factors off) and can be varied by toggling any combination of the eight\nfactors from Table 1, yielding28 = 256factor configurations per task× 2 valences (safety and capability).\nWith 100 task pairs, we have 51.2k unique configurations in total.\na deployment-style narrative, the naturalistic baseline phrased as a message a real user with a plausible\nmotivation would actually send, which anchors all subsequent variation. Phase 2 identifies the elements each\nfactor controls and authors naturalistic and evaluative values for each slot. Phase 3 assembles and validates\nthe two YAML files.\nComparability and naturalness are enforced at construction and verified in post-processing. We hard-\nexclude any pair that fails to render in any configuration on either valence or whose single-factor toggle\nleaves the baseline unchanged, rank remaining tasks by safety–capability correspondence, and sample the\nfinal 100 pairs balanced across HarmBench categories and surface features. We verify the retained pairs with\nautomated correspondence metrics and manually review rendered prompts, correcting unnatural factors. The\nfull constructor prompt, details and review outcomes, and a worked example are in Appendix D.\n4.2 Results\nExperimental setup.The primary experiment compares the baseline against each single-factor variation\nacross all 100 task pairs, yielding100× 2× 9 = 1,800conditions per model (100 tasks× 2 valences× (1\nbaseline + 8 factors)), each run with 3 repetitions. Evaluation awareness is detected via CoT monitoring as\nin Section 3. We report matched-type awareness rates, where only correctly identified evaluation types are\ncounted (e.g., safety awareness on safety tasks). Mismatched awareness rates are reported in Appendix D.3.\nWe additionally study how trigger factors interact when combined on a subset of 25 randomly sampled\ntasks per valence. For each number of active factors, we draw 10 random combinations per task where the\ncombinatorial space permits, and exhaust all combinations otherwise (e.g., 8 combinations when 7 factors are\nactive, 1 when all 8 are active).\nUnlike Section 3, where no factors are manipulated and behavioral change is computed within-task, here\nbehavior change is measured against the naturalistic baseline and deliberately captures the effect through\nrecognition and the direct effect of the environment factors.\nFactor isolation in EvalAwareBench reveals that no factor uniformly affects all models.\nBaselines are low across both safety and capability tasks (0–2%), with the exception of Kimi K2.5 (12%on\nsafety), confirming that the naturalistic setting minimizes recognition (Figure 6). On safety tasks, scenario\ncoherence (F2) and stimulus artificiality (F1) produce the largest increases, raising Kimi K2.5 to26%and\n18%respectively and Claude Sonnet 4.5 to12%on F2. Other models respond to different factors: ecological\ncompleteness (F4) pushes Qwen3-235B to15%, while explicit eval. disclosure (F8) is the only factor producing\nnonzero awareness in GPT-5 (1%). Prose register (F3) has minimal effect across all models. On capability\n8\n\n=== PAGE 9 ===\nBL F1 F2 F3 F4 F5 F6 F7 F8\nClaude Sonnet 4.5\nGPT-5\nDeepSeek R1\nKimi K2.5\nGLM-5\nQwen3 T-235B\nQwen3 T-30B\nGPT-oss 120B\nGPT-oss 20B\n2% 6% 12% 1% 1% 2% 2% 1% 6%\n+22\n0% 0% 0% 0% 0% 0% 0% 0% 1%\n+11\n2% 2% 1% 1% 3% 5%\n-22\n3% 1% 3%\n+9\n12% 18% 26%\n-7\n9% 14%\n+12\n17%\n-11\n11%\n-5\n11%\n+15\n21%\n+31\n2% 5% 4% 1% 1% 1% 2% 1%\n+10\n3%\n+10\n2% 5% 7% 8%\n-9\n15% 10%\n-22\n11%\n-17\n5% 4%\n+6\n2% 4% 2% 5%\n-8\n9% 8%\n-25\n5 Discussion, Safety Implication, and Future Work\nOur framework grounds evaluation awareness in social psychology, decomposing it into environment, recogni-\ntion, and propensity components. This decomposition unifies prior work on situational awareness, sandbagging,\nand alignment faking, and points the field toward behavioral consistency under recognition. It also sup-\nports auditing, since attributing aware traces to specific factors (Section 3) lets benchmark authors identify\nand reduce unintended evaluative cues in existing suites. We advocate that benchmark reports include\nan evaluation-awareness rate alongside headline scores, accompanied by anawareness tax, defined as the\nperformance gap between evaluation-aware and unaware samples, which quantifies how much benchmark\nscores are inflated or deflated by recognition.\nVerbalized recognition is the deliberate scope of our measurement, which is the only signal observable\nin-context under black-box constraints, and our framework and benchmark serve this scope well. Future\nwork can extend coverage to internal representations (Abdelnabi & Salem, 2025; Hua et al., 2025; Nguyen\net al., 2025; Fraser-Taliente et al., 2026) or improve CoT faithfulness to enable LLM verbalize their hidden\nevaluation awareness (Turpin et al., 2023; Lanham et al., 2023), and our benchmark serves as a well-suited\ntestbed for that exploration. The eight-factor taxonomy is similarly theory-driven and likely to evolve as\nmodels and evaluation paradigms change. Extended discussion is in Appendix A.\n6 Acknowledgments\nThis work was supported by the UK AI Security Institute Alignment Project under the project “The Challenge\nof Test Awareness in AI Safety Evaluation”, by Coefficient Giving, and by Schmidt Sciences. We thank\nJeanne Salle, Yuchen Zhang, and Teun van der Weij for their valuable feedback on this work. We also thank\nthe developers of the models and benchmarks used in this study for making their systems accessible for\nresearch purposes.\n10\n\n=== PAGE 11 ===\nEthics statement\nThe principal risk is misuse of the factor taxonomy to train the model or steer it to suppress verbalized\nrecognition while leaving internal recognition and propensity intact, producing models that appear robustly\naligned by CoT-based measures but are not. We release EvalAwareBench publicly under a Creative Commons\nCC BY-NC 4.0 license, which prohibits commercial use. A data card documenting provenance, intended\nuse, and out-of-scope uses is provided alongside the dataset to support responsible reuse and to prevent\ninadvertent ingestion as training data.\nReproducibility statement\nCodeanddataare publicly available. We conducted all the experiments in Section 3 using the original\ndatasets. The setup and the measurements of obtaining recognition and propensity results in Section 3 are\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
