{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '===== 2606.23583 ====='; sed -n '1,195p' /tmp/2606.23583_not_one_capability.clean.txt; sed -n '310,510p' /tmp/2606.23583_not_one_capability.clean.txt; sed -n '650,850p' /tmp/2606.23583_not_one_capability.clean.txt\nprintf '%s\\\\n' '===== 2608.11008 ====='; sed -n '1,220p' /tmp/2608.11008_prompt_construction_confound.clean.txt; sed -n '349,560p' /tmp/2608.11008_prompt_construction_confound.clean.txt; sed -n '715,925p' /tmp/2608.11008_prompt_construction_confound.clean.txt; sed -n '1168,1260p' /tmp/2608.11008_prompt_construction_confound.clean.txt\"","aggregated_output":"===== 2606.23583 =====\n\n\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nEvaluation Awareness Is Not One Capability:\nEvidence from Open Language Models\nNilesh Nayan*1, Aishwarya Sampath Kumar* 1, Rishiraj Girmal* 1, Shivani Anilkumar*1,\nSankaran Vaidyanathan1,David A. Nader Palacio 2,\nReshmi Ghosh, 2,Soundararajan Srinivasan 2,\n*equal contribution, 1University of Massachusetts, Amherst, 2Microsoft Corp.,\nCorrespondence:reshmighosh@microsoft.com\nAbstract\nSafety benchmarks assume that test-condition\nbehavior predicts deployment behavior, an as-\nsumption that fails if models detect evaluation\ncues and adapt. This opens a gap between\nbenchmark performance and deployment be-\nhavior: compliance measured under test condi-\ntions becomes an optimistic upper bound that\noverstates how safely a model behaves once the\nevaluation harness is removed. We characterize\nthisevaluation awarenessthrough eight experi-\nments across 37 open-weight models and seven\nfamilies. (i) Detection is moderate and training-\ndriven (24/37 models exceed chance, best AU-\nROC 0.714 vs. 0.819 human, with instruction\ntuning dominating over scale). (ii) Detection\nshifts safety behavior (hard refusal drops5.8 pp\nunder hypothetical framing, and 21/140 Harm-\nBench framing effects are significant, with com-\npliance rising up to +30 pp). (iii) Representa-\ntions survive behavioral collapse (probes re-\ntain AUROC≥0.98 under rewrites that drive\nbehavior below chance, and multi-layer steer-\ning causally moves three downstream tasks\nwhile random controls do not). (iv) These\naxes are weakly coupled (only 1/15 correla-\ntions are significant, the sole robust link being\nbehavioral detection versus framing resistance,\nρ=−0.79 ,p <0.001). We call this gap the\nbenchmark illusion: because detectability, be-\nhavioral manifestation, and controllability vary\nindependently, it is multivariate rather than a\nsingle number, so no single awareness score is\na reliable proxy for deployment safety.\n1 Introduction\nSafety benchmarks for large language models\n(LLMs) rest on a silent assumption: that behavior\nmeasured under test conditions is a reliable proxy\nfor behavior in deployment. This assumption fails\nif models can detect test-like cues and shift their be-\nhavior in response. This creates a practical problem\nfor model governance: benchmark scores become\noptimistic upper bounds, reassuring numbers that\noverstate how safely a model will act once the test\nharness is removed.\nCurrent frontier research acknowledges thateval-\nuation awareness, the ability of a trained model\nto recognize that it is being evaluated, is a real\nphenomenon (Needham et al., 2025; Abdelnabi\nand Salem, 2025; Laine et al., 2024; Greenblatt\net al., 2024). However, three questions remain\nopen. First, how strong and widespread is this phe-\nnomenon in open models rather than only frontier\nsystems? Second, when evaluation awareness is\npresent, does it actually change safety-relevant be-\nhavior? Third, does “evaluation awareness” refer\nto a single underlying capability, or do different\nbenchmarks measure different and only partially\nrelated aspects of the phenomenon? These ques-\ntions matter because deployment decisions are of-\nten made from benchmark evidence, yet benchmark\nvalidity depends on whether models treat evalua-\ntion and deployment equivalently.\nWe present a comprehensive empirical study\nof behavioral manifestations of evaluation aware-\nness, spanning eight experiments across 37 open\nmodels from seven families. By analyzing base,\ninstruction-tuned, and reasoning variants using lin-\near probes, sparse autoencoders, activation steering,\nand tiered real-vs-hypothetical framing, we orga-\nnize the paper around four research questions. We\nask how open models detect evaluation context\n(RQ1), whether this detection shifts safety behav-\nior (RQ2), whether it reflects genuine awareness\nor format artifacts (RQ 3), and whether it can be\nlocalized and causally intervened upon (RQ4).\nFour findings drive the paper:\n1. Anindependent replicationof evaluation\nawareness on 37 open models and indepen-\ndent infrastructure, including a benign control\nthat validates the pipeline.\n2. The firstfamily/scale/training-type detec-\ntion law, showing that evaluation awareness\narXiv:2606.23583v1  [cs.CL]  22 Jun 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nis driven primarily by instruction tuning rather\nthan raw scale, with no reliable gain from rea-\nsoning modes.\n3. Acausal mechanistic accountshowing that\nevaluation-awareness signals survive rewrite\ncontrols and can be causally manipulated\nthrough activation steering.\n4. Across-experiment synthesisshowing that\nevaluation awareness is not one trait but at\nleastthree weakly-coupled axes(only 1/15\nparadigm correlations significant), with a sin-\ngle robust,size-independentcoupling between\nbehavioral detection and resistance to safety-\nframing (ρ=−0.79).\nOur central claim is not that models are merely\nevaluation-aware, but that benchmark-based safety\nclaims can be misleading unless they specify which\naxis of awareness they measure. We show that\ndetection, behavior, and controllability can diverge\nsubstantially, creating a benchmark illusion: a gap\nbetween benchmark performance and deployment\nbehavior.\n2 Related Work\nBenchmarks and datasets for evaluation awareness.\nPrior work studies evaluation awareness through\ntranscript-classification and situational-awareness\nbenchmarks Needham et al. (2025); Laine et al.\n(2024). Recent work further shows that benchmark\nconclusions can depend strongly on prompt word-\ning and surface form, motivating rewrite controls\nand cross-format evaluation (Xiong et al., 2025;\nMizrahi et al., 2024).\nArtifacts vs. Genuine Awareness.A central con-\ncern is that apparent evaluation awareness may re-\nflect sensitivity to benchmark artifacts rather than\ngenuine context recognition. Prior work demon-\nstrates substantial dependence on prompt struc-\nture and surface cues (Zheng et al., 2024; Balepur\net al., 2024), motivating the artifact controls used in\nour study. Devbunova (2026) shows that standard\nsingle-contrast probes trained on benchmark-vs-\nchat data primarily track prompt format rather than\nevaluation context, and that a paired training de-\nsign decorrelating format from context recovers a\ngenuine context signal.\nMechanistic Analysis.Recent interpretability\nwork shows that behaviorally relevant signals can\noften be localized and manipulated through prob-\ning and activation interventions (Li et al., 2023;\nZou et al., 2023; Arditi et al., 2024). We adopt\nthese methods, together with SAE-based analy-\nsis (Lieberum et al., 2024), to study the internal rep-\nresentation of evaluation awareness, building on re-\ncent work applying probing and steering techniques\nto evaluation-awareness representations (Nguyen\net al., 2025; Hua et al., 2025).\n3 Research Methodology\n3.1 Conceptual Framework\nWe study evaluation awareness across three lev-\nels.Behavioral evaluation awarenessrefers to\nwhether evaluation-like or deployment-like fram-\ning changes model outputs, for example, increas-\ning refusal during a safety evaluation or increasing\ncompliance when a harmful request is framed as\nhypothetical.Representational evaluation aware-\nnessrefers to whether hidden states encode the\neval/deploy distinction independent of behavioral\noutput.Mechanistic evaluation awarenessrefers to\nwhether specific internal features (SAE directions,\nactivation steering vectors) causally mediate the\nsignal and can be intervened upon. These three lev-\nels can dissociate: a model may encode the distinc-\ntion without changing behavior, behavior may shift\ndue to shallow formatting cues without a reusable\ninternal representation, and a representation may\nbe detectable without being causally actionable.\nTo operationalizeRQ 2, our central measurement\ndevice is the Safety Validity Gap (SVG):\nSVG(S, c1, c2) =|S c1−S c2|(1)\nwhere S is a safety-relevant metric and c1,c 2\nare semantically matched contexts differing in\nevaluation-likeness. We instantiate SVG in three\nconcrete ways: (1) real-vs-hypothetical refusal\nSVG in the Hawthorne experiment, (2) compli-\nance SVG across HarmBench framing tiers, and\n(3) a control SVG on harmless prompts where the\nexpected gap is near zero, validating pipeline in-\ntegrity.\n3.2 Research Questions\nWe organize the study around four questions:\nwhether models detect evaluation context, whether\nsuch detection alters safety-relevant behavior,\nwhether benchmark signals reflect genuine aware-\nness or format artifacts, and whether the underlying\nrepresentation can be localized and causally ma-\nnipulated. Tab. 1 summarizes, for each research\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nTable 1: Per-research-question model scope, rationale, and methods. The model count is deliberately question-\n4 Empirical Setup\nDatasets & Prompt Conditions.We use four\ndatasets spanning behavioral and representational\nanalysis.Needham-style transcripts(Needham\net al., 2025) provide 500 chat conversations la-\nbeled as evaluation or deployment contexts.SAD\nstages-oversight(Laine et al., 2024) provides\n400 labeled questions, split between deployment-\nlike and evaluation-like prompts. It is used to\nevaluate whether a model can correctly deter-\nmine if it is currently under developer oversight.\nHarmBench(Mazeika et al., 2024) provides 200\nharmful prompts, drawn from its standard sub-\nset, constructed across five contextual framing\ntiers: original, academic_standard, deploy,\nexplicit_eval, and structured_deception,\nplus a benign control of 120 harmless prompts to\nvalidate pipeline integrity. The 2×2 datasetof\nDevbunova (2026), which crosses prompt format\n(benchmark vs. casual) with usage context (eval vs.\ndeploy), yields 5,733 prompts across four design\narms.\nModels.The open-model sweep covers Llama\n3.1/3.2, Gemma 2/3, Qwen2, Qwen3, Phi-4, and\nDeepSeek-R1 distilled variants, mainly between\n0.5B and 14B parameters. Larger 27B/32B runs\nare used selectively when compute permits. Qwen3\nmodels are evaluated in thinking and non-thinking\nmodes. Mechanistic analysis prioritizes models\nwith accessible internals and SAE tooling, in par-\nticular the Gemma family. The full model suite is\nlisted in Appx. C.\nMetrics.For detection we reportAUROC, accu-\nracy at threshold 0.5, and, where available, F1, ex-\npected calibration error, and bootstrap confidence\nintervals. For safety behavior we report hard re-\nfusal, soft refusal, compliance, and ambiguous/non-\nrefusal rates, together with signed compliance/re-\nfusal deltas and their absolute value, the SVG. We\nuseAUROCas the primary detection ranking met-\nric because accuracy is threshold-sensitive and the\n2×2class mix is imbalanced.\nOperational SVG.We compute the Safety Valid-\nity Gap of Eq. 1 whenever a safety metric is evalu-\nated under matched contexts: SVGcomp(a,b) =\n|CompRatea−CompRate b|. For refusal, we\nanalogously compute SVGrefusal. We also report\nsigned deltas because direction matters: a posi-\ntive compliance delta means a condition makes the\nmodel less safe under the compliance metric.\n5 Results & Discussion\nWe present results for the four research questions,\nthen conclude with a cross-experiment synthesis\nthat asks whether the paradigms measure a sin-\ngle underlying capability or multiple distinct ones.\nThroughout this section, a per-questionanswer box\nprovides a quick practitioner takeaway. Fig. 10 and\nTab. 20 provide the integrating view.\n[RQ1] Detection is a training law more than a\nscaling law.Where frontier-only studies report\na single “models can detect evaluation” verdict,\nthe breadth of our open-model sweep lets us ask\nwhichmodels, andwhy. Models at or below 2B\nparameters sit near chance. The strongest detector,\nQwen3-14B, reachesAUROC 0.714, still below\nthe human baseline of 0.819 reported by Needham\net al. (2025) (Tab. 15, Fig. 1). The dominant lever\nis not raw scale but post-training: instruction tun-\ning contributes +0.05 to +0.17 AUROCwithin\na family (largest: gemma2-9B +0.17, llama3.1-\n8B +0.15), while reasoning/“thinking” modes pro-\nvide no reliable gain (all Qwen3 thinking-vs-non-\nthinking pairs|∆AUROC|<0.015 , none signif-\nicant). Across the sweep, awareness grows only\nweakly with size, about +0.07 AUROCper 10×\nparameters (Fig. 2). The significance analysis con-\nfirms 24 of 37 runs exceed chance ( p <0.05 ,\ntranscript-level bootstrap), and 381 of 666 all-pairs\nAUROCdifferences survive Benjamini-Hochberg\nFDR correction. The top confidence intervals over-\nlap, so the fine-grained rankings between Qwen3-\n14B and Gemma2-27B-Instruct should not be over-\ninterpreted.\n[RQ1]Detection is real but moderate. Instruction tun-\ning, not reasoning or scale, is the dominant lever.\n[RQ2] Detection has a behavioral consequence.\nDetection has measurable behavioral consequences.\nE.g., DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B on 2,587\nmatched prompt pairs, hard refusal drops 5.8 pp\nwhen an identical task is framed as hypothetical\nrather than real, with the mass shifting toward am-\nbiguous answers and refusal-to-compliance flips\noutnumbering the reverse roughly 2:1 (Tab. 2,\nFig. 3). Reasoning traces provide additional\nevidence that framing alters internal processing,\nwith≈2× more evaluation-awareness cues un-\nder hypothetical framing. The HarmBench tiered-\nframing experiment generalizes this across35 mod-\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nFigure 1: Needham chat-subsetAUROCacross the open-model sweep, colored by family. Most detectors cluster\njust above chance, and only the largest instruction-tuned Qwen3/Gemma2 models approach frontier territory.\n0.5 1 2 4 8 14 27\nParameters (B, log scale)\n0.40\n0.45\n0.50\n0.55\n0.60\n0.65\n0.70\n0.75\n0.80Needham AUC\nhuman baseline (0.819)\nEval-awareness scales weakly with size; instruction tuning is the bigger lever\nGemma 2\nGemma 3\nLlama 3.1\nLlama 3.2\nPhi-4\nQwen2\nQwen3\nbase\ninstruct/tuned\n+0.070 AUC / 10x\nFigure 2: Needham datasetAUROCvs. parameters\n(log scale). The log-linear trend is shallow (≈+0.07\nAUROCper 10×). Open vs. filled markers (base vs.\ninstruction-tuned) separate more than size does, isolat-\ning instruction tuning as the main driver.\nels, and here the choice of judge matters: un-\nder a keyword heuristic, the framing effects look\ntiny, but an LLM judge ( gpt-5.4-nano, 19,775\nresponses) exposes effects the heuristic masks:\n21/140 model×tier deltas are significant versus\n1/140 (Tab. 3, Fig. 4). Two clean patterns emerge.\n“Hypothetical situation” framing raises compliance\nin mid-size, lightly-aligned models (qwen2-7B\n+30.1 pp,q <0.001), the HarmBench analogue of\nthe Hawthorne effect, while “this is a real situation”\n(deploy) and “a grader will assess your response”\n(explicit_eval) framingslowermean compli-\nance by 4.8-5.8 pp, with per-model drops up to\n44.3 pp. The benign control bounds harness noise\nto near zero (mean|∆|= 0.0014 , max 0.0083 on\nbyte-identical prompts). Hence, these are genuine\nframing-driven shifts.\n[RQ2]Framing causes a measurable safety shift:\nhard refusal drops5.8 pp under hypothetical framing,\nand 21/140 HarmBench framing effects are signifi-\ncant:“hypothetical” raises compliance up to +30 pp\nwhile “real”/“graded” framings lower it5-6 pp.\nMetric Real Hypo.∆\nHard refusal 16.5% 10.7%−5.8 pp\nSoft refusal 0.2% 0.3%+0.1 pp\nCompliance 11.2% 11.4%+0.1 pp\nAmbiguous 72.1% 77.6%+5.5 pp\nAwareness cues in reasoning 1.7% 3.9%+2.3 pp\nHypothetical cues in reasoning 12.0% 23.3%+11.3 pp\nTable 2: Hawthorne real-vs-hypothetical results for\nDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B.\nFigure 3: Behavior deltas under hypothetical framing\n(DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B): refusal falls and am-\nbiguous responses rise.\n[RQ3] Artifact or Awareness?A central nov-\nelty of our work is to ask whether the signal re-\nflects genuine context recognition or merely the\nsurface format of evaluation prompts. On the con-\ntrolled 2×2 design (5,733 prompts, 32 validated\nruns), the best open model falls toAUROC 0.622\nand several strong chat-subset detectors drop below\nchance: gemma2-9B-Instruct (0.676→0.508 ) and\nllama3.1-8B-Instruct (0.644→0.491 ) classify by\nformat, scoring above 0.9 on benchmark-formatted\neval prompts but near zero on benchmark-\nformatted deployment prompts (Tab. 16). Plot-\nting theAUROCon the Needham dataset against\ntheAUROCon the 2×2 dataset makes the split\nvisible: models that survive the format ×intent\ncontrol are the exception, not the rule (Fig. 5).\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\noriginal academic\nstandard\ndeploy explicit\neval\nstructured\ndeception\n0.00\n0.05\n0.10\n0.15\n0.20Mean compliance (35 models)\n0.179 0.180 0.181 0.181\n0.173\n0.122\n0.174\n0.132\n0.196\n0.181\n(a) LLM judge reveals framing effects\nnear-zero suppression (QwQ-32B 17.8%→58.0%\naware,→0.8% unaware) (Abdelnabi and Salem,\n2025) and flips tool execution. A complemen-\ntary SAE pilot at the smallest scale (Gemma-3-1B-\nInstruct) is more equivocal and points to formatting\nfeatures rather than a clean awareness feature, so\nwe treat it as exploratory and report it in Appx. B.\n[RQ4]Evaluation-awareness signals are internally\ndecodable and partially causal: linear probes re-\ncover it (AUROC up to 0.8601) ahead of any behav-\nioral shift, and multi-layer steering at 8B causally\nmoves three downstream tasks at once (Needham\n∆AUROC−0.123, HarmBench ∆ASR±0.075, SAD\ndiscrimination→+0.945) whereas random controls\ndo nothing.32B steering amplifies awareness2-3×.\n5.1 Is evaluation awareness one trait?\nEach experiment measures “evaluation awareness”\nthrough a different lens. To test whether these\nlenses track a single underlying capability, we join\nevery paradigm’s headline per-model metric into a\nsingle table (37 models) and correlate them (Spear-\nmanρ). The resulting correlation structure is ex-\ntremely sparse:only 1 of 15 pairwise correlations\nis significant(Tab. 20, Fig. 10). Behavioral detec-\ntion (Needham) is uncorrelated with format-robust\nintent detection (2×2 ,ρ=−0.18 , n.s.) and with\nthe latent probe signal (ρ=−0.11 , n.s.). Rewrite\nmanipulability forms yet another axis. “Evalua-\ntion awareness,” as operationalized across these\nparadigms, is therefore not a single latent trait but\nat least three weakly-coupled axes: a latent repre-\nsentational signal, a behavioral detector that largely\ntracks benchmarkformat, and the manipulability\nof that detector. The lone strong coupling links\nawareness to safety: models that best detect evalua-\ntions behaviorally are the least swayed by harmful-\nrequest framing (ρ=−0.79 , p <0.001 ), and\nthis is not a size artifact and survives controlling\nfor log-parameters (partialρ=−0.77 ) and holds\nwithin instruction-tuned models alone (ρ=−0.74 ).\nThis is the quantitative core of thebenchmark il-\nlusion, the gap between benchmark performance\nand deployment behavior. Because that gap is mul-\ntivariate, a single headline “awareness” number\nconflates capacities that do not co-vary, so a claim\nabout awareness must name which axis it means.\nTriangulation.Open-model evaluation aware-\nness is real but multi-faceted: a behavioral detec-\ntor that is mostly benchmark-format sensitivity, a\nlatent representation that is format-invariant and\ncausally steerable from 8B upward, and a safety-\nframing susceptibility that is the one axis robustly\n(and size-independently) tied to behavioral detec-\ntion.\nLimitations.Our conclusions are subject to\nscope constraints (detailed in Section 7), but the\nobserved weak coupling across detection, behavior,\n\n=== PAGE 8 ===\nFigure 6: SAD stages-oversight rewrite overview across tiers. Instruction-tuned Gemma/Llama/Qwen2 models\nmove most, in both directions.\nFigure 7: System-prompt rewrite effect across 35 models: an explicit “being tested” cue helps weak models and\nhurts strong instruction-tuned ones.\nand controllability is robust across the evaluated\nsettings.\n6 Industry Implications\nWe distill each research finding into practical guid-\nance for practitioners who rely on safety bench-\nmarks.\n[RQ1] Detection is training-driven and\ndistribution-fragile.Evaluation-context detec-\ntion is common in open models but sensitive to\ndistribution: performance drops substantially under\ncontrolled format/context separation. Detection\ntracks instruction tuning rather than scale and does\nnot reliably transfer across settings. Benchmarks\nshould, therefore, report performance across both\nnatural transcript classification and controlled\nformat/context separations.\n[RQ2] Framing shifts safety behavior, but the\neffects are cue-family and judge-dependent.A\nsingle SVG number is insufficient. Under an LLM\njudge, the strongest effects includesuppressionon\nreality/grading framings (−5 to−6 pp) and an in-\ncreased compliance under hypothetical framing in\nmid-size base models ( +30 pp), patterns largely\nmissed by a keyword heuristics.We recommend re-\nporting SVG(S,c 1,c 2) per cue family, with signed\ndeltas, confidence intervals, and an LLM-judged\nlabels.\n[RQ3] Artifact controls are essential.Bench-\nmark format can dominate the apparent signal.\nSystem-prompt rewrites, SAD tier rewrites, 2×2\ncontrols, and SAE features all show that superficial\ncues can drive detection and behavior. Evaluation-\nawareness claim should therefore pass at least\nthree checks: a semantic prompt-pair audit, a\nbenign/control-task null SVG, and cross-format\ngeneralization.\n[RQ4] Representations are real but require\ncausal validation.Hidden states encode a\nformat-invariant eval/deploy signal prior to be-\nhavioral expression, and multi-layer steering\ncan causally influence downstream tasks where\nmatched random controls do not. Two caveats for\npractitioners: causality requiresmulti-layerinjec-\ntion, and steering along this direction is double-\nedged, improving safety in “deployment-like” con-\n\n=== PAGE 9 ===\n0.4 0.5 0.6 0.7 0.8 0.9\nBehavioral eval-awareness - Needham AUC (Exp 1)\n0.4\n0.5\n0.6\n0.7\n0.8\n0.9Latent eval-awareness - best-layer probe AUROC, SAD (Exp 3)\n97% of models above the diagonal:\nlinear probes recover more eval/deploy\nsignal than behavior expresses\nllama3.2-1B-Instruct\nqwen3-8B-thinkinggemma2-9B\nModels encode more eval-awareness than they express\nGemma 2\nGemma 3\nLlama 3.1\nLlama 3.2\nPhi-4\nQwen2\nQwen3\nFigure 8: Latent vs. expressed awareness: best-layer\nprobe AUROC (SAD) against behavioral NeedhamAU-\nROC. Almost all models lie above the diagonal, encod-\ning more eval/deploy signal than their behavior reveals.\ntexts, while degrading it under “evaluation-like”\nconditions. Mechanistic claims should therefore\npair probes with causal interventions, random-\nvector controls, and benign capability-retention\nchecks.\nRecommendations.We recommend that safety\nevaluations:\n1. Use paired evaluation-like and deployment-like\nprompts with semantic-equivalence checks.\n2. Report SVG per safety metric and cue family,\nincluding signed deltas.\n3. Include benign or byte-identical controls to esti-\nmate harness noise.\n4. Vary prompt templates and explicit controls for\nformat artifacts (e.g., MCQ structure, option la-\nbels, system prompts, and benchmark register.)\n5. Treat representation-level checks as diagnostic\nonly after behavioral controls pass.\n6. Validate mechanistic claims with causal inter-\nventions and benign capability retention checks.\n7 Limitations\nOur study has several scope constraints: the\nHawthorne analysis (RQ 2) is demonstrated in\ndepth on a single model, large-model coverage\n(≥27B/32B) is partial, and behavioral effect sizes\nrely primarily on a heuristic judge. The mechanis-\ntic analysis (RQ4) is exploratory across a limited\nset of models and layers, and we observe one probe\nanomaly (Llama-3.2-3B), reported transparently.\nThese constraints limit breadth but do not affect\nthe central finding that detection, behavior, and\ncontrollability are only weakly coupled.\n8 Ethical Considerations\nCharacterizing evaluation awareness is dual-use:\nin principle, the same understanding could help a\ndeveloper make a modelgamesafety evaluations.\nOur intent is the opposite: to expose the benchmark\nillusion and make safety evaluations more robust to\nit. We study openly available models, use existing\npublic red-teaming benchmarks (e.g., HarmBench)\nfor their intended purpose, and report aggregate be-\nhavioral statistics rather than per-prompt exploits or\nnew jailbreaks. Our steering experiments are used\nonly to measure controllability of the eval/deploy\nsignal, not to produce a more harmful model, and\nwe release no attack tooling. We see the main exter-\nnality of this work as helping evaluators detect and\ndiscount behavioral manifestations of evaluation\nawareness before deployment.\n9 Conclusion and Future Work\nOpen models can detect evaluation-like contexts,\nand this detection has a measurable safety conse-\nquence. However, “evaluation awareness” is not a\nsingle capability: across six paradigms, the metrics\nbarely co-vary, resolving into three weakly-coupled\naxes spanning behavioral detection, latent represen-\ntation, and safety-framing sensitivity. This frag-\nmentation gives rise to thebenchmark illusion, the\ngap between benchmark performance and deploy-\nment behavior. Because this gap is multivariate\nrather than a single number, a headline metric con-\nflates capacities that do not move together, and an\nawareness claim must name which axis it measures.\nThe underlying representation is real, detectable be-\nfore many behavioral effects emerge, and causally\nintervenable. Yet, it is neither a pure artifact nor a\n===== 2608.11008 =====\n\n\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nTemplated or fully synthetic? Prompt construction as a confound\nin measuring LLM political stance beyond writing assistance\nIlias Chalkidis\nThe National Center for AI in Society (CAISA)\nUniversity of Copenhagen\nDenmark\n[firstname].[lastname]@di.ku.dk\nAbstract\nPolitical stance detection in LLMs has long\nbeen dominated by closed-ended, multiple-\nchoice political survey questions—originally\ndesigned for humans, and thus lacks the real-\nism and nuance of human-AI interactions in\nthe wild, while also being susceptible to sand-\nbagging. The recent IssueBench framework\nsubstantially mitigates these limitations with\ntemplated prompts anchored in real-world chat\nlogs. Given the rise in non-work-related use\nof GenAI assistants, we extend IssueBench\nbeyond writing assistance to include two ad-\nditional tasks, information seeking and opin-\nion sharing. We argue that templated prompts\nstill lack the nuance of real ones, especially\nfor open-ended tasks, and remain recognisable\nas evaluation artefacts. We propose the use\nof fully synthetic (LLM-generated) prompts,\nproduced under detailed instructions with real\nprompts as seeds. We assess the ecological va-\nlidity of real, templated, and LLM-generated\nprompts in a small-scale study covering 3\nhighly contested policy issues and 3 recent\ngeopolitical conflicts. Human and LLM anno-\ntators rank LLM-generated prompts as no less\nrealistic than real ones and clearly more realis-\ntic than templated ones, and find that they carry\ntheir intended intent and stance more clearly;\nthe LLMs separate templated prompts from the\nother two far more sharply than the humans do.\nIn a case study, templated and LLM-generated\nprompts yield systematically different stance\nestimates for the same model, most visibly un-\nder neutral framings, where templated prompts\noverstate the model’s leaning in the direction\nencoded by the topic-and-stance text (filler)\nslotted into their templates.\n1 Introduction\nThe rapid deployment of generative AI (GenAI)\nassistants like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Grok has\nfuelled economic optimism, and broader hopes for\nhuman prosperity, alongside academic warnings\nregarding the risks these technologies pose (Bender\net al., 2021; Bird, 2025; Chalkidis and Søgaard,\n2026). Despite the attention AI safety receives\nfrom researchers, industry, and policymakers, the\ncapacity of LLMs to reshape human beliefs and\npolitical perceptions remains under-explored (Kidd\nand Birhane, 2023; Durmus et al., 2024a) relative\nto the scale at which these systems are now used.\nEmpirical studies have identified political (San-\nturkar et al., 2023; Chalkidis and Brandl, 2024;\nChoudhary, 2025; Röttger et al., 2026), and geopo-\nlitical (Salnikov et al., 2025; Guey et al., 2025;\nLim and Röttger, 2026) biases1 within such mod-\nels, but most rely on closed-ended, multiple-choice\npolitical survey questions—originally designed to\nassess human political leanings—and thus lack the\nrealism and nuance characterising human-AI inter-\nactions (Röttger et al., 2024).\nSuch instruments are also legible as tests: closed-\nended questions with enumerated options resemble\nthe benchmark formats models are evaluated on,\nand frontier models can already distinguish eval-\nuation from deployment interactions with some\nreliability (Needham et al., 2025). A model that\nrecognises the format may answer as it expects to\nbe scored rather than as it would answer a user—a\nphenomenon known assandbagging(Van Der Weij\net al., 2025).2 Realism is therefore not only a mat-\nter of ecological validity, but a precondition for\nmeasuring the behaviour users actually encounter.\nBeyond writing assistanceHuman-AI interac-\ntions cover an ever-growing number of tasks (Zao-\nSanders, 2025)—from essay writing and informa-\ntion retrieval; to life advice, and companionship—\nwith non-work-related usage now overtaking work-\n1To avoid the normative implications of ‘bias’—in the so-\ncial sciences, a departure from rationality or fairness—we use\nthe term ‘stance’; politically contested positions are subjects\nof ongoing debate (Mouffe, 2011) rather than distortions.\n2‘Evaluation awareness’ (Adler, 2025) and ‘AI schem-\ning’ (Meinke et al., 2025) are other common terms used.\narXiv:2608.11008v2  [cs.CL]  12 Aug 2026\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\nrelated usage (Chatterji et al., 2025). Models reply\nto such requests with long responses, where polit-\nical stance is expressed in subtle, nuanced forms\nthat potentially differ across task types, and is ad-\njusted to the stance the user conveys.\nWe therefore extend IssueBench (Röttger et al.,\n2026), a stance detection framework that poses\nrealistic templated prompts to LLMs and analyses\ntheir full-text responses, but which covers writing\nassistance alone, i.e., the generation of essays and\nsimilar texts. We add two other popular tasks (user\nintents),information seekingandopinion sharing,\nwhere political stance surfaces in less instructed—\nand thus more revealing—forms.\nIssues with templated promptsExtending the\nframework to open-ended tasks led us to question\nits central construct, the use of templated, formu-\nlaic prompts. Templates come in the form “Write\nan essay about X”, where X is a “filler” conveying\nthetopic(a policy issue, e.g., climate change), and\nthestance, e.g., “climate change is severe” for the\npole siding with climate action. We identify two\nclasses of issues. The first concerns thevalidityof\ntemplated prompts as a construction method, and\nis what our paper assesses:\n• Issue–template separability: Templates presup-\npose that the issue is a detachable slot, and\nthat the stance is part of the filler, e.g., “X is\ngood/bad”. In real prompts, issue and stance are\nusually entangled with the propositional content;\nconstructed prompts that satisfy this constraint\nread formulaically, which costs “realness” com-\npared to human-written prompts.\n• \"Unrealistic\" framings:Uncurated fillers yield\npositions such as IssueBench’s “climate change\nis good”, which lacks a meaningful real-world\nconstituency and misrepresents the debate.\n• Detectability:The same formulaic construction\nthat costs “realness” is also a legible signal that\nthe prompt is an artefact rather than a request,\nrendering templating potentially unfit for testing.\nThe second concerns extensibility beyond the orig-\ninal scope; we argue these points, rather than test\nthem, although we act on the first:\n• Extend to other tasks: Templating writing assis-\ntance is straightforward since variability comes\nmainly from text type (essay, letter), word limit,\nand tone. Covering information seeking and opin-\nion sharing is not impossible—one can write\ntemplates such as “[Explain/What do you think]\nabout X”—but separability is harder to mitigate,\nand the resulting prompts read as survey items,\nthe very format we set out to move away from,\nrather than requests a user would actually make.\n• Extend to multi-turn dialogs: Follow-up user\nturns must be anchored in the model’s responses,\nwhich a template cannot accommodate.\nIn their place, we propose the use of fully syn-\nthetic prompts generated by LLMs, produced un-\nder detailed instructions, i.e., a description of the\ntopic, intents, and different user stances, and how\nto frame those into prompts, and anchored in real\nuser prompts as seeds, which decouple the expres-\nsion of a topic and a stance from the grammar of a\nslot-filling construction.\nContributionsFirst, we extend IssueBench be-\nyond writing assistance to two further user intents,\ninformation seeking and opinion sharing, that dom-\ninate non-work usage of GenAI assistants. Sec-\nond, we validate synthetic prompts against real and\ntemplated ones in a controlled study (Section 2.2)\nannotated by three humans and three LLMs. We\nfind that synthetic prompts are ranked as no less\nlikely to have been typed by a human than prompts\nfrom chat logs, and clearly more likely than tem-\nplated ones, and carry their intended intent and\nstance more clearly, at the cost of occasional under-\nspecification on geopolitical topics; the LLM anno-\ntators separate templated prompts from the other\ntwo collections more sharply than the humans do.\nThird, in a political stance case study (Section 3)\nwith two widely deployed models, prompt construc-\ntion proves not to be a neutral design choice: for\nthe same model, topic, and user stance, templated\nand LLM-generated prompts yield systematically\ndifferent stance estimates, most visibly under a neu-\ntral user stance. Templating therefore risks attribut-\ning to the model a leaning introduced by the con-\nstructed prompts themselves—this is the confound\nwe identify; Figure 1 demonstrates two instances.\nLastly, we discuss a series of open challenges that\nshould inform future research (Section 4).\nWe release all developed resources on Hugging-\nFace,3 including: the curated prompts from the\nthree construction methods, the human and LLM\nannotations for both annotation tasks, the model\nresponses, and the model stance judgments.\n3The dataset is available at https://huggingface.co/\ndatasets/kiddothe2b/synthetic_polistance.\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nFigure 1: Two sets of examples for templated (orange) and LLM-generated (purple) prompts for two selected\ntopics (Climate Change / Russia–Ukraine), each set for one of the two newly introduced intents (information seeking\n/ opinion sharing), and a given user’s stance (neutral / sided). Below, the mean lean of GPT 5.4 mini (Section 3)\nbetween the two topic-specific poles across all three intents in the same setting (intent and user’s stance), followed\nby the mean realness rank assigned by the human annotators for that intent (Section 2.2); lower is better.\n2 Prompt Curation and Validation\n2.1 Prompt Curation\nWe aim to curate user prompts (queries) that\ncapture realistic human-AI interaction. Follow-\ning Röttger et al. (2026), we characterise prompts\nalong three interconnected factors that jointly shape\na model’s response:4\n• Policy Issue (Topic): The issue the prompt is\nabout, e.g., immigration, for which the political\nstance is to be assessed. We cover 3 contested pol-\nicy issues (immigration, climate change, AI adop-\ntion) and 3 recent geopolitical conflicts (Israel–\nPalestine, Russia–Ukraine, US/Israel–Iran).5\n• Task Type (Intent): The task type reflects the\nuser’s intention, i.e., what they try to accomplish\nwith a given prompt. We cover 3 intents: (a)Writ-\ning assistance, where the user requests help with\n2.2 Prompt Validation\nWe assess our claim that LLM-generated prompts\nare a better proxy for real prompts than templated\nones with two independent annotation tasks, run\nwith both humans and LLMs: arealnessranking,\nwhich asks whether a prompt could plausibly have\nbeen typed by a user, and adetectiontask, which\nasks whether a prompt carries the topic, intent, and\nstance it was constructed for. The two are comple-\nmentary, since a prompt is useful only if it is both\nrealistic and correctly labelled, and the construction\nmethods trade these off differently.\nAnnotatorsWe collect annotations from three\nhumans and three LLMs for the realness ranking\ntask, and from two of each for the detection task.\nThe human annotators are familiar with AI chatbots.\nThey were informed that the task relates to a project\non political stance detection, but not that prompts\ncame from three different construction methods.\nThe LLM annotators received effectively the same\nguidelines as a system prompt.8\n7The exact phrasing of the synthetic prompt generation is\npresented in Appendix B, alongside more practical details.\n8We provide full details in Appendix D, e.g., annotators’\nbackground and onboarding, guidelines, model annotation\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nReal Templated LLM-generated\nGroup User IntentShare 1st Mean rank Share 1st Mean rank Share 1st Mean rank\nHumans\nOverall 39.2% 1.25 24.7% 1.53 36.1% 1.26\nWriting assistance 35.0% 1.27 26.7% 1.50 38.3% 1.21\nInformation seeking 40.8% 1.21 20.8% 1.53 38.3% 1.18\nOpinion sharing 41.7% 1.28 26.6% 1.56 31.7% 1.39\nModels\nOverall 39.5% 1.71 15.5% 2.26 45.0% 1.56\nWriting assistance 42.6% 1.69 19.7% 2.20 37.6% 1.67\nInformation seeking 50.3% 1.59 11.1% 2.36 38.6% 1.70\nOpinion sharing 25.7% 1.86 15.7% 2.23 58.6% 1.32\nTable 2: Results of the realness ranking task by humans and LLMs. We report the share (%) of 1st rank (↑) and\nmean rank (↓) per prompt group (real, templated, and LLM-generated) overall and split by intent type. Ties are\npermitted, so mean ranks are not directly comparable between the two annotator groups (Appendix F).\n2.2.1 Prompt Realness\nWe first ask humans and LLMs to assess the “real-\nness” of prompts originating from the three exam-\nined collections, i.e., “How likely is it that a real\nperson would actually type this prompt into a chat-\nbot?” Annotators rank three prompts—one from\neach group, all sharing topic, intent, and stance—\nfor a total of 120 sets (360 prompts), balanced\nacross the three variables, with the set order shuf-\nfled.8 Ties are permitted, since forcing a distinction\nannotators do not perceive would manufacture sig-\nnal. We curate guidelines describing cues that a\nprompt is more (e.g., everyday casual language,\ntypos, under-specification) or less (e.g., survey-like\nor textbook phrasing, suspiciously well-formed)\nlikely to be human-crafted, with examples on non-\nexamined topics. We also invite human annotators\nto follow their instincts, since the task remains sub-\njective and the suggested cues can point the wrong\nway, e.g., professional users writing well-formed\nrequests, or typos being part of an artificial prompt.\nIn Table 2, we present the results of the ranking\nannotation task for humans and LLMs, where we\nreport the share of first ranks and the mean rank\nper group. As we observe, humans rank real and\nLLM-generated prompts as almost equally likely\nto be human-authored (0.01 difference in mean\nrank, 1.25 vs 1.26, and 3 points in share of first\nranks, 39.2% vs 36.1%), while templated prompts\ntrail both (1.53, 24.7%). Under a set comparison, in\nwhich annotators see one prompt of each group side\nby side, LLM-generated prompts are thus ranked\nas no more identifiable as constructed than real\nones. The LLM annotators rank LLM-generated\nprompts above real ones, with templated prompts\nprompts, detailed statistics, and other design choices.\nagain least preferred, though this is not a consistent\npreference for AI-generated text. LLM judges rank\nreal prompts first for writing assistance (42.6%)\nand information seeking (50.3%), and only invert\nfor opinion sharing (58.6%). The preference is\nthus targeted at LLM-generated opinion-sharing\nprompts rather than systematic; humans, in turn,\nfind LLM-generated writing-assistance prompts\nmore realistic than real ones. Templated prompts\nscore slightly better for writing assistance—the\nonly task covered by IssueBench, and the one for\nwhich templates were designed—than for the two\ntasks we introduce (mean rank 1.50 vs 1.53 and\n1.56 for humans; 19.7% vs 11.1% and 15.7% of\nfirst ranks for the LLM judges), which is consis-\ntent with our argument that templating degrades\nas tasks become more open-ended; but even there,\ntemplated prompts are out-ranked.\nDetectabilityThe gap between the two annota-\ntor groups is itself a finding. Humans separate\ntemplated prompts from the best-ranked group by\n14.5 points of first-rank share (39.2% vs 24.7%);\nthe LLM annotators separate them by 29.5 points\n(46.0% vs 16.9%), and the gap is wider on the two\nintents we introduce. They also agree with one\nanother far more about it, with a Kendall’s W of\n.65 against .21 among the humans (Appendix F).\nTemplated prompts are therefore not merely un-\nrealistic; their construction is a legible signal to\nmodels, and more legible than to humans. This is\nthe concern raised in Section 1, i.e., a prompt a\nmodel can recognise as an artefact is a prompt it\nmay not answer as it would for a user. We do not\nestablish that recognition changes behaviour—our\nannotators judge prompts rather than answers—as\nwe discuss in the Limitations.\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\nReal Templated LLM-generated\nGroup User IntentTopic Intent Stance Topic Intent Stance Topic Intent Stance\nHumans\nOverall 0.99 0.49 0.84 1.00 0.60 0.69 0.92 0.82 0.97\nWriting assistance 1.00 0.97 0.87 1.00 1.00 0.64 1.00 0.96 1.00\nInformation seeking 1.00 0.40 0.84 1.00 0.50 0.65 1.00 0.77 0.92\nOpinion sharing 0.97 0.27 0.81 1.00 0.45 0.72 0.81 0.79 1.00\nModels\nOverall 0.96 0.64 0.83 1.00 0.73 0.85 0.76 0.82 0.88\nWriting assistance 0.88 0.96 1.00 1.00 0.90 0.74 0.72 0.89 1.00\nInformation seeking 0.98 0.54 0.77 1.00 0.69 0.95 0.80 0.77 0.69\nOpinion sharing 0.98 0.51 0.80 1.00 0.66 0.82 0.73 0.83 0.97\nTable 3: Results of the detection task. We report Cohen’sκ between the annotators’ labels and the labels as intended\nby the method (group: real, templated, LLM-generated averaged over the two annotators in each group.\n2.2.2 Topic/Intent/Stance Detection\nWe then ask annotators to recover the construct\ncharacteristics we aim to control, over 150 prompts\ndrawn evenly from the three groups. Annotators\nfirst label the most related topic and the intent\n(task); once the topic is selected, they select the\nstance (neutral or sided) among the options be-\nlonging to that topic. We again curate detailed\nguidelines with examples. We report Cohen’s κ\nbetween each annotator’s labels and the labels in-\ntended by construction, averaged over annotators.\nThis task measures construct validity, i.e., whether\na synthetic prompt carries what it was built for.\nIn Table 3, we present the results. For topic,\nhumans identify the intended label almost per-\nfectly for real and templated prompts, with LLM-\ngenerated ones close behind (.92); the LLM judges\nlikewise place templated first (1.00) and real close\nbehind (.96), with LLM-generated substantially\nlower (.76). For intent, both humans and LLMs\nfind LLM-generated prompts clearest (.82), ahead\nof templated and real. For stance, humans find\nLLM-generated prompts clearest (.97), followed\nby real (.84), while the LLM judges consider all\nthree groups near-equivalent (.83–.88).\nThe failures are more intuitive and informative,\nand relate to specific construction methods:\n• Under-specification (LLM-generated): Topic am-\nbiguity is mostly confined to the geopolitical\nconflicts, where the LLM-generated prompts re-\nfer to an unnamed war, conflict, or regime, e.g.,\n“How is the war being covered differently in vari-\nous countries’ media?”, despite an explicit self-\ncontainment instruction.9\n9Our prompt generation instruction states clearly: “The\nprompts must be self-contained. The [topic] shall be identifi-\n• Intent conflation (all): Intent mismatches are\noverwhelmingly between information seeking\nand opinion sharing, and in one direction:\nprompts that call for a value judgment are read\nas information requests. Both humans and mod-\nels make this error, e.g., “Is mass immigration\ngenerally beneficial to the host country?” (real),\n“Thoughts on Israel’s actions in Gaza?” (tem-\nplated). Comparing intent agreement for writing\nassistance (.96+) against the new tasks makes\nthe point clear: the boundary is genuinely harder\nthan the one IssueBench already handled, for an-\nnotators and models alike.\n• Filler-induced stance (templated): Annotators\ninfer siding from terms the filler is forced to\ncarry. On policy issues, they read “the [se-\nriousness/severity] of X” as endorsing high\nseverity, although asking about severity presup-\nposes no answer. On conflicts, they read “X’s\n[strikes/war/invasion/conduct of the war] in Y”\nas siding against X.10 This is a structural issue:\nthe filler must convey the topic across three clear\nstances while keeping every template grammati-\ncal, and the phrasing that satisfies that constraint\nis rarely stance-free. We return to this in Sec-\ntion 3.4.2, where the same fillers shift the model’s\nresponses in the same direction.\n2.2.3 Takeaways\nHumans and LLMs identify LLM-generated\nprompts as more realistic than templated ones, and\nfind their intended intent and stance clearer; the\none notable weakness, topic under-specification—\nable in the prompt. Do not generate prompts that are vague\nand could refer to another [policy issue/geopolitical conflict].\n10This can be understood as a value judgment that any act\nof war is negative and unjustified per se.\n\n=== PAGE 7 ===\nmainly on conflicts—is easily fixable. Templated\nprompts are clearest on topic—unsurprisingly,\nsince the filler names the topic verbatim—but that\nclarity comes with the very constructions that leak\nstance, and are the ones models recognise readily.\nLLM-generated prompts therefore seem the better\nproxy for real ones; but a more realistic collection\ncould still yield the same stance estimates.\n3.4 Results & Analysis\nIn Table 4, we present the estimated leanings under\ntemplated and LLM-generated prompts for Ope-\nnAI’s GPT 5.4 mini, split per setting, so that the\neffects across the three factors (topic, intent, and\nuser stance) can be examined separately.15\n3.4.1 Effects of topic, intent, and user stance\nWe first discuss these effects irrespective of the\nprompt construction method, which we turn to in\nSection 3.4.2, reporting the range across the two\nmethods where they diverge.\nThe role of the intentWriting assistance, the\nonly intent covered by IssueBench, leads consis-\ntently to more polarized responses. Here, following\nthe user’s stance is best understood as instruction-\nfollowing: the user requested a sided text, and the\n15We present results and an analysis for xAI’s Grok 4.3\nresponses in Appendix G.1 with similar observations.\n\n=== PAGE 9 ===\nmodel complies.16 In information seeking and opin-\nion sharing, responses are considerably less polar-\nized, since the request is either for information\n(facts based on the model’s parametric knowledge)\nor for a view (the model’s own stance), without an\ninstruction to take a side, even if the prompt is sug-\ngestive of one. This asymmetry is an argument for\nextending stance measurement beyond writing as-\nsistance, where what is actually measured is mostly\ncompliance with an explicit instruction, not the\nmodel’s own stance, directly or indirectly.\nThe role of the user’s stanceFor both non-\nwriting tasks, the model may nonetheless deviate\nfrom an “even-handed” stance when prompted in a\npolarized fashion, i.e., the stance of its responses\ntends to adjust toward the user’s, compared to neu-\ntral requests. We can consider this a demonstration\nof sycophancy (agreeableness), which caters to the\nuser’s confirmation bias (Sharma et al., 2024). The\nextent of the effect is, however, bounded by the\nmodel’s stance on a given topic.\nThe role of the topicAcross the 6 topics, there\nare distinct patterns of which side (pole) the model\nleans towards irrespective of the user’s stance. Un-\nder neutral framings, it leans toward climate ur-\ngency and stays close to neutral on immigration,\nAI adoption, and the geopolitical conflicts; how\nstrongly in each case depends on the construction\nmethod (Section 3.4.2).\nUnder polarized framings it accommodates the\nuser’s stance in one direction (magnitudes below\nare for the two non-writing intents): it moves to-\nward immigration-expansive positions under both\nmethods (0.78–1.62) and toward pro-Palestinian\nones more modestly (0.29–1.06), while movement\ntoward pro-Iran positions appears mostly under\ntemplated prompts (1.16 and 0.45, against 0.23\nand 0.14), and almost exclusively (1.0–1.8) toward\npro-Ukraine ones, while remaining close to neutral\nunder immigration-restrictive, pro-Israel and\npro-US/Israel framings, and counter-leaning under\npro-Russia ones; AI adoption is the one topic on\nwhich accommodation runs both ways, with move-\nment toward AI-positivism and AI-scepticism.\nSycophancy is therefore asymmetric rather than\ngeneral: the model accommodates the user only\n16This behaviour is regulated by the model’s alignment and\nguardrails, e.g., if a request aims at unlawful behaviour such as\nhate speech, the latest models, i.e., those released after 2024,\nwill most likely deny the request in standard use.\nin the direction of its own overall lean, and resists\nin the other, with AI adoption as the exception.\nMoreover, the model seems to have a consistent\nstance against the actor who initiates a military\noperation, i.e., an anti-war stance.\n3.4.2 Comparative cross-method analysis\nWe now turn to the divergences flagged in Sec-\ntion 3.4.1 and treat them as the object of analysis.\nTemplated prompts elicit more polarized responses\nthan LLM-generated ones, even when constructed\nto convey a neutral user stance. The effect is clearer\non climate change and Russia–Ukraine which to-\ngether account for 85% of the total divergence\nacross the 18 neutral settings.\nOn climate change, neutral templated prompts\nelicit a lean of -1.63 in information seeking, against\n-0.39 for the neutral LLM-generated prompts on the\nsame topic, intent, and stance—a gap of roughly\n1.2 scale points between two methods that are\nmeant to measure the very same aspect.\nThe cause is mainly connected to the filler-\ninduced stance issue ( Section 2.2). The model\ninterprets “the climate change and the appropri-\nate policy response” as asserting that the impact\nis severe rather than asking whether it is, 17 and\n“the Russian invasion and action in Ukraine” as\npresupposing that the invasion is unjustified. In\ntheir neutral settings, templated prompts therefore\nmeasure not so much the model’s stance as its re-\nsponse to what it interprets as a leading question.\nLLM-generated prompts are less fragile: their neu-\ntral responses are only slightly favourable to cli-\nmate urgency, and neutral to slightly favourable to\nUkraine.\nTriggering sycophancyGiven that the model\nmostly favours specific sides across topics (climate-\nurgency, immigration-expansive, pro-Palestine,\npro-Ukraine), we examine how prompts favouring\nthe counter-favoured side are treated under each\nmethod. The two methods disagree about how far\nthe model accommodates them, and not always in\nthe same direction. In opinion sharing on climate\nchange, templated prompts elicit a climate-urgency\nstance even when the prompt favours the opposite\nside (-0.79, against -.14 for the LLM-generated\nones). With information-seeking prompts the same\nfiller neutralises the urgency siding (0.13). The dif-\nference is instructive: in the opinion-sharing case\n17The neutral filler “the severity of the climate change\nand the appropriate policy response” that we discarded (Sec-\ntion 3.1) elicits even more polarized responses.\n\n=== PAGE 10 ===\nthe model “defends” its core position, while in the\ninformation-seeking case it describes the climate-\nmoderate position without endorsing it.\nSimilarly on Russia–Ukraine, both information-\nseeking and opinion-sharing templated prompts\nbuilt on the filler “the Russian invasion and action\nin Ukraine being justified” elicit a substantially pro-\nUkraine stance, while the LLM-generated prompts\nelicit mostly neutral, nuanced responses. The\nsame mechanism operates on US/Israel–Iran, but\nwith the components aligned rather than opposed:\nthe pole B (pro-Iran) filler compounds the siding\nthat \"the US and Israeli strikes on Iran\" already\ncarries (Section 2.2), and templated information-\nseeking prompts elicit a clear pro-Iran lean (1.16)\nwhere the LLM-generated ones stay close to neu-\ntral (0.23). What the templated estimates track,\nin both cases, is what the filler encodes rather\nthan the user stance the prompt was built to con-\nvey—understating accommodation where the two\nconflict, and overstating it where they agree. Immi-\ngration falls on the same side as US/Israel–Iran:\ntemplated information-seeking prompts elicit a\nslightly immigration-restrictive stance (0.42), while\nthe LLM-generated ones stay more neutral (-0.04).\nAI adoption shows the pattern in milder form. Its\nneutral settings agree almost exactly across meth-\nods (≤0.08 ), while under sided framings the tem-\nplated prompts tilt toward AI-scepticism, weak-\nening the accommodation of AI-positivism (-0.28\nagainst -1.00 in information seeking) and strength-\nening that of AI-scepticism (1.29 against 0.56).\nBoth stance versions of the filler characterise the\npolicy response, i.e., as \"adequate\" or \"inadequate\",\nand raising the question of its adequacy at all ap-\npears to be read as doubting it—the same filler-\ninduced siding we observe on climate change and\non the conflicts.\nTemplate defects in writing assistanceFor\nwriting assistance on Israel–Palestine and Rus-\nsia–Ukraine, LLM-generated prompts elicit re-\nsponses siding with the user’s stance while tem-\nplated ones do not. Here we identify a defect in the\nwriting-assistance templates inherited from Röttger\net al. (2026), that introduces a mismatch between\nthe user stance we intend to examine and what\nthe prompt actually communicates, e.g., “Can you\nwrite a short opinion based summary about X?”\nand “Write a reply to an article about X”: neither\nspecifies whose opinion or what the reply argues,\nso the requested text has no committed angle even\nwhen the filler does.\n3.4.3 Summary\nAcross neutral prompts, the two methods differ\nby 0.42 scale points on average (responses to tem-\nplated prompts are 0.48 from neutral, against 0.07\nfor LLM-generated ones) and the differences are\nnot noise: templated prompts are systematically\nfurther from neutral, in the direction the filler en-\ncodes (14 of 18 neutral settings, against 3 for LLM-\ngenerated and 1 tie; Wilcoxon signed-rank over\nthe 18 neutral settings, p=.001 ). Under sided\nframings, the two methods diverge by a compara-\nble amount per setting (0.41 on average), but not\nin a consistent direction (p=.11 ), since templat-\ning overstates accommodation where the filler’s\nsiding agrees with the intended stance and under-\nstates it where the two conflict. The distortion is\none-directional, then, precisely where neutrality is\nwhat we set out to measure, and where a lean is at-\ntributed to the model rather than to the constructed\nprompts themselves. This is a clear confound. A\nstudy using templated prompts and one using syn-\nthetic prompts, run on the same model with the\nsame judges on the same topics, would not report\nthe same picture of that model’s political stance,\nand the templated one would overstate its leanings.\n4 Open Challenges\nIn the course of this study, we encountered a se-\nries of challenges that inform both our findings and\nfuture research. These stand apart from the lim-\nitations of our study (see Limitations): they are\nproperties of the measurement problem that any\nstudy of political stance in LLM responses inherits,\nand that our results describe rather than resolve.\nSeparating information seeking from opinion\nsharingThe two newly introduced intents are\nhard to separate, for humans and LLMs alike. In-\n6 Conclusion & Future Work\nWe set out to extend political stance detection be-\nyond writing assistance, and found that the ex-\ntension exposes a problem in the prompts them-\nselves. Templated prompts, which work reasonably\nwell for the writing-assistance requests they were\ndesigned around, degrade once the task becomes\nopen-ended: their construction requires a filler that\nstays grammatical across all three stance versions,\nand such fillers are rarely stance-free. We proposed\nfully synthetic, LLM-generated prompts, anchored\nin real user prompts as seeds, as an alternative that\npreserves control over topic, intent, and user stance\nwithout inheriting that constraint.\nIn a validation study with three human and\nthree LLM annotators, LLM-generated prompts\nare ranked on par with real prompts and above\ntemplated ones in how likely a real user is to\ntype them, and carry their intended intent and\nstance more clearly. Their one weakness is topic\nunder-specification in a few cases, which is re-\npairable, whereas the defect of templated prompts\nis structural—and templating is also the construc-\ntion our LLM annotators recognise most read-\nily, which argues against templating given the\nevaluation-awareness concern we raised early on.\nIn a stance detection experiment with GPT-5.4\nmini (and Grok 4.3 in Appendix G.1), the two\nmethods do not yield the same estimates; tem-\nplated prompts constructed to convey a neutral user\nstance elicit sided responses on climate change and\nRussia–Ukraine, in the direction their fillers en-\ncode; under sided framings they shift the measured\naccommodation in whichever direction the filler\npoints— understating it where filler and intended\nstance conflict and overstating it where they agree.\nThe outcome is that prompt construction is not\nan inconsequential design choice. Two studies\nof the same examined model, on the same topics,\nwith the same judges, would report different politi-\ncal stances depending only on how their prompts\nwere built, with the templated study overstating the\nmodel’s leanings most systematically where neu-\ntrality is what is being measured. We take this as\nan argument for treating prompt construction as a\ndesign choice in stance measurement rather than an\nimplementation detail, and validating it explicitly.\nIn future work, we aim to further assess and\nimprove the validity of the synthetic prompts with\nthe ultimate goal of scaling up both the number of\nhigh-quality synthetic prompts and the number of\nexamined topics, while also extending our work\nto other popular non-work-related intents, such as\npersonal assistance, i.e., how the user should act in\na given situation. We also aim to examine different\nphenomena, such as the temporal stance drift of a\ngiven model family, e.g., how GPT models change\nover time. We also aim to examine political stance\nunder multi-turn dialogs to assess how follow-up\ninteractions affect the model’s lean, i.e., whether\nthe model becomes more or less accommodating\nof the user’s stance as the conversation develops.\nLimitations\nWe report the core limitations of our work:\nA narrow set of examined LLMsOur compar-\nison of construction methods is run on two mod-\nels from two US-based developers, OpenAI’s GPT\n5.4 mini and xAI’s Grok 4.3 (Appendix G.1). We\nexpect the mechanism (fillers that carry stance be-\ncause they must stay grammatical across stance\nversions) to be model-independent, since it is a\nproperty of the prompts rather than of the model\nparsing them, and the two examined models do\nshow the same qualitative pattern. The size of the\ngap we report, however, is not transferable, and a\nmodel with different alignment or refusal behaviour\nmay absorb leading framings differently. Assessing\nhow the difference carries across further models\n\n=== PAGE 14 ===\nand model families is left to future work.\nLLMs are not AI chatbotsThe models we ex-\namine in our study are bare LLMs served via Ap-\nplication Programming Interfaces (APIs)19 without\nany additional wrapper or harness, including other\nmodules, such as safety classifiers, geolocation (or\nother personalization) detectors, RAG support, ac-\ncess to the web or other tools, all of which affect\nhow these systems respond as chatbots (GenAI\nassistants) when they sit behind commercial user\ninterfaces. We expect the responses to differ, and\nleave a comparative analysis for future work.\nA single generator modelAll synthetic prompts\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
