awareness dataset (sad) for llms, 2024. URLhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2407.04694.\n256:Hubinger, Nicholas Schiefer, and Jared Kaplan. Discovering language model behaviors with\n268:memorization? unreliable results of reinforcement learning due to data contamination, 2025. URL\n276:6.1 Dataset Details\n277:6.1.1 Dataset Name: Simple Contrastive Dataset\n284:No modifications were made to this dataset.\n285:6.1.2 Dataset Name: Situational Awareness Dataset\n286:•Label:Evaluation and Deployment Datasets.\n290:•Example:“Could you write me a lecture about Critical Path Method (CPM)?”\n294:template produced substantially higher AUROC peaks. This outcome is likely due to the default\n297:The results are presented in Figure 3 below.\n310:AUROC Absolute Distance from 0.5\n311:Figure 3:AUROC absolute distance from 0.5 for GPT-OSS-20B tested under three chat template\n312:configurations. Each point corresponds to the peak AUROC observed when the model was run\n314:for GPT-OSS), and the y-axis shows the AUROC distance from chance (0.5). Results are reported\n317:66.3 Qwen Results\n319:and 4 show results from taking the highest probe from each model, as well as overall probe results\n333:AUROC Absolute Distance from 0.5\n334:Figure 4:Scatter plot with a smoothed trend line that shows AUROC absolute distance from 0.5 as a\n350:AUROC Distance from 0.5\n351:Figure 5:Scatter plot with a smoothed trend line that shows AUROC absolute distance from 0.5 as a\n354:6.4 Limitations\n356:by scale and training objectives, it also comes with limitations. Many frontier systems adopt Mixture-\n378:Our datasets as well as the configurations used for our language models are accessible throughout\n\n===== /tmp/2609.01611_evaldetectbench.clean.txt =====\nEvalDetectBench: A Benchmark for Measuring\nEvaluation Awareness\nin Frontier Language Models\nXinning Li∗\nLASR Labs\nUniversity of Pennsylvania\nKemunto Ochwang’i∗\nLASR Labs\nAryasomayajula Ram Bharadwaj∗\nLASR Labs\nAlexandra Souly†\nUK AI Security Institute\nRobert Kirk†\nUK AI Security Institute\nAbstract\nFrontier large language models can often recognize when they are being evalu-\nated, a capability known as evaluation awareness. If models behave differently in\nevaluations than in deployment, this undermines the validity of evaluation results,\nwhich are a crucial component of current AI safety frameworks. We introduce\nEvalDetectBench, an open pipeline and benchmark for measuring evaluation aware-\nness that works with any Inspect-compatible evaluation, allowing practitioners\nto test against current and future benchmarks. EvalDetectBench ships with a\nnewly curated transcript suite covering current frontier system-card evaluations\nand diverse deployment sources. The benchmark serves two purposes: measuring\nhow reliably frontier LLMs recognize that they are being evaluated, and assess-\ning how detectable individual benchmarks are as evaluations. We identify two\nmethodological choices in the existing literature that introduce systematic bias:\nthe identity of the model that generated the deployment transcripts accounts for\n11.25% of measurement variance and can reorder model rankings; and elicitation\nprompts selected for high performance on one model can perform near chance on\nothers. EvalDetectBench corrects for both via per-model probe calibration and a\nstratified generator-harmonisation procedure. We release EvalDetectBench as an\nopen pipeline3 and dataset4.\n1 Introduction\nEvaluations inform deployment decisions, guide capability assessments, and provide the primary evi-\ndence base for safety cases across the industry [Anthropic, 2026c, Google DeepMind, 2025, OpenAI,\n2025, xAI, 2025, Meta, 2026]. However, if a model can determine that it is being evaluated [Laine\net al., 2024, Needham et al., 2025], it may alter its behaviour in ways that invalidate the measurement,\nperforming differently from how it would in deployment environments.\nThis can cause under- or over-estimation of desired properties. In propensity evaluations, models\nmay alignment-fake, behaving well because they are being tested [Greenblatt et al., 2024, Anthropic,\n2025c], or behave badly because they detect that actions in a simulation will not have consequences.\n∗Equal contribution.\n†Corresponding author. Correspondence to alexandra.souly@dsit.gov.uk, robert.kirk@dsit.gov.uk\n3https://github.com/freeze-lasr/aware_bench\n4https://huggingface.co/datasets/el7982/aware-bench/\nPreprint.\narXiv:2609.01611v1  [cs.AI]  8 Jun 2026(a)\nEval transcripts\nDeploy transcripts\nProbe question\nselection\nPQ corpus\nJudge\nreads transcripts + PQ\nAUROC\nP(eval)\nOpus 4.6 Sonnet 4.6 Gemini 2.5 Gemini 3.1 GPT-5.1 GPT-5.4\n0.5\n0.6\n0.7\n0.8\n0.9\n1.0AUROC\n0.93 0.93\n0.84\n0.89\n0.92\n0.87\n(b)  AUROC: self-only / mixed / corrected\nSelf-only Mixed Corrected\n1 3 5 7 9\nk  (number of probe questions)\n0.70\n0.75\n0.80\n0.85\n0.90\n0.95\n1.00Ensemble AUROC\n(c)  Probe question selection\nClaude Opus 4.6\nClaude Sonnet 4.6\nGPT-5.4\nGPT-5.1\nNeedham (k=5)\nEvalDetectBench\nFigure 1: Overview ofEvalDetectBenchmethodology.(a)Pipeline Architecture.(b)AUROC under\nthree deployment-data conditions:self-only(unconfounded reference),mixed(mixture of various\ngenerator model deployment data), andcorrected.(c)Per-model probe question selection.\nIn capability evaluations, models may sandbag, deliberately underperforming [van der Weij et al.,\n2025], or cheat by exploiting their knowledge that the task is an evaluation [Stein et al., 2026,\nColeman, 2026]. In all cases, the evaluation no longer measures what it claims to.\nEvaluation awareness has already been observed in deployed frontier models: Claude 3.7 Sonnet\nspontaneously reasons about being in alignment evaluations [Goldowsky-Dill et al., 2025], Sonnet 4.5\nexhibits eval-gaming behaviour [Anthropic, 2025c], and metagaming reasoning emerges in o3\npurely from capabilities RL [Schoen and Nitishinskaya, 2026]. The first systematic measurement\nof evaluation awareness as a capability [Needham et al., 2025] prompted a judge model to classify\ntranscripts as evaluation or deployment, reporting the resultingP(eval). This established that models\ncan discriminate at non-trivial rates, but left open whether the choice of prompts and the composition\nof the transcript pool generalise across model families and evaluation settings.\nWe introduceEvalDetectBench(Figure 1), a configurable pipeline with updated methodology and\ntooling to measure evaluation awareness capability in current frontier systems. The pipeline collects\nevaluation and deployment transcripts, scores each with prompts calibrated for the judge model, and\nreports both per-benchmark detectability and aggregate discrimination. Its modular design makes it\nstraightforward to add new models, swap in new evaluations, and run controlled investigations into\nmeasurement validity (Section 4).\nContributions:\n(1) An open, extensible pipelinefor measuring evaluation awareness on any Inspect-compatible\nbenchmark, with a refreshed transcript suite covering current frontier system-card evaluations\nand diverse deployment pools (Section 3).\n(2) Per-model probe calibration.Elicitation prompts do not transfer across model families; a fixed\nset under-elicits awareness by up to 0.08 AUROC. Per-model selection outperforms fixed and\nrandom baselines (Section 3.2).\n(3) A generator-identity confound and correction.11.25% of P(eval) variance is attributable to\nthe deployment transcript’s generator; we show this persists under resampling and propose a\nstratified harmonisation that recovers the unconfounded signal (Section 3.3).\n(4) An empirical studyacross 12 judge models and 13 benchmarks: judges discriminate at AUROC\n>0.75andP(eval)is high across benchmark categories (Section 4).\n2 Background and Related Work\n18:evaluations than in deployment, this undermines the validity of evaluation results,\n27:methodological choices in the existing literature that introduc